### The manual: one entry per command, with its summary (first sentence,
### reused by the NAME line and by whatis), its description and its attributes:
###   .synopsis     how to write it (technical identifiers are not translated)
###   .option-X     the explanation of option -X
###   .example-N    the explanation of the N-th example (the command is in the code)
### See src/engine/commands/manual.ts.

man-a2dismod = disables an Apache module: removes its links from /etc/apache2/mods-enabled (see a2enmod). Then restart Apache (systemctl restart apache2). A directive of the module still present in the configuration makes it invalid: apache2ctl configtest says so. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2dismod module…
    .example-1 = stop listing the contents of directories without an index page
man-a2disconf = disables an Apache configuration snippet: removes its link from /etc/apache2/conf-enabled (see a2enconf). Then reload Apache. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2disconf conf…
    .example-1 = disable the CGI scripts configuration
man-a2dissite = disables an Apache site: removes its link from /etc/apache2/sites-enabled (the file stays in sites-available). Then reload Apache. The 000-default site, installed with the package, answers any request whose name matches no other site on the same port: it is often disabled. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2dissite site…
    .example-1 = disable the default site
    .example-2 = then reload Apache
man-a2enconf = enables an Apache configuration snippet (a .conf file in /etc/apache2/conf-available, read for every site) by creating its link in conf-enabled. Then reload Apache. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2enconf conf…
    .example-1 = the available snippets
    .example-2 = enable /etc/apache2/conf-available/servername.conf
man-a2enmod = enables an Apache module, such as mod_rewrite (rewriting addresses) or mod_headers: creates in /etc/apache2/mods-enabled the links to its .load (LoadModule) and .conf files from mods-available, and first enables the modules it depends on. A directive whose module is not loaded makes the configuration invalid ("Invalid command", "perhaps misspelled or defined by a module not included in the server configuration"); in an .htaccess, the page answers 500. Then restart Apache (systemctl restart apache2). apache2ctl -M lists the loaded modules. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2enmod module…
    .example-1 = enable mod_rewrite
    .example-2 = then restart Apache
    .example-3 = the loaded modules
man-a2ensite = enables an Apache site: creates, in /etc/apache2/sites-enabled, a link to its file in /etc/apache2/sites-available. The file name must end in .conf: that is what IncludeOptional sites-enabled/*.conf in apache2.conf reads (a link without .conf, made by hand with ln -s as for nginx, is ignored). Then reload Apache (systemctl reload apache2). Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = a2ensite site…
    .example-1 = the available sites
    .example-2 = enable sites-available/site.conf
    .example-3 = then reload Apache
man-ansible = runs an Ansible module on machines from the inventory, without a playbook (an "ad-hoc" command). The pattern says which: all, a group, a host, several separated by commas. -m gives the module (command by default), -a its arguments ("name=htop state=present", or the command for command and shell), -b runs it with sudo (become). Ansible connects over ssh, never asking a question: the host key must be in ~/.ssh/known_hosts and the account (remote_user in ansible.cfg, -u, or ansible_user) must have the key. The configuration is read from ./ansible.cfg (the current directory), else ~/.ansible.cfg, else /etc/ansible/ansible.cfg. Each host answers SUCCESS (nothing changed), CHANGED, FAILED or UNREACHABLE.
    .synopsis = ansible PATTERN [-m module] [-a arguments] [-b] [-i inventory] [-u account] [--list-hosts]
    .option-m = the module (ping, apt, copy, service…).
    .option-a = the module's arguments.
    .option-b = with sudo (become).
    .option-i = the inventory to read.
    .option-u = the account for the ssh connection.
    .option-l = only these hosts (limit).
    .option-C = only checks, changing nothing (check).
    .option-D = shows the differences (diff).
    .example-1 = reach the three centres
    .example-2 = run a command everywhere
    .example-3 = reload ssh on one centre, with sudo
    .example-4 = who is in the centres group
man-ansible-doc = the documentation of Ansible's modules: -l lists them, one line each; ansible-doc MODULE gives its options (= in front of the required ones) and examples. The ansible.builtin. prefix is optional.
    .synopsis = ansible-doc -l ; ansible-doc MODULE
    .option-l = the list of modules.
    .example-1 = the modules
    .example-2 = the copy module's options
man-ansible-inventory = shows the inventory as Ansible understands it: --graph the tree of groups and their hosts, --list everything, in JSON, with each host's variables. The inventory is an INI file: one [group] per section, one host per line (with its variables: ansible_host=…, ansible_user=…).
    .synopsis = ansible-inventory --graph ; ansible-inventory --list
    .example-1 = the groups and their hosts
    .example-2 = everything, in JSON
man-ansible-playbook = plays a playbook: a YAML file, a list of plays; each play has hosts, and tasks that each call a module, in order, on each host. become: true goes through sudo. A task that changes something can notify a handler (notify), played only once at the end of the play. The output says, for each task and each host, ok (nothing to change), changed, skipping or fatal; PLAY RECAP sums it up. A good playbook run again says changed=0 (idempotence). --check plays without changing anything, --diff shows the differences in files, --limit restricts to some hosts, --syntax-check only checks the YAML.
    .synopsis = ansible-playbook PLAYBOOK [--check] [--diff] [--limit PATTERN] [--syntax-check] [--list-hosts] [--force-handlers]
    .option-i = the inventory to read.
    .option-l = only these hosts (limit).
    .option-C = only checks, changing nothing (check).
    .option-D = shows the differences (diff).
    .option-b = with sudo (become), for all plays.
    .option-u = the account for the ssh connection.
    .example-1 = play the playbook
    .example-2 = the rehearsal: what would change, without changing anything
    .example-3 = one centre first
    .example-4 = check the YAML
man-apache2ctl = checks Apache's configuration and drives the service (under systemd, start, stop, restart and graceful go through systemctl). The configuration starts from /etc/apache2/apache2.conf, which loads the modules (mods-enabled), the ports (ports.conf: Listen, the addresses and ports Apache listens on), then the snippets (conf-enabled) and the sites (sites-enabled/*.conf). A site is a <VirtualHost address:port> block with ServerName (its name), ServerAlias (its other names) and DocumentRoot (the directory of the pages); a <Directory path> section sets up a directory (Require all granted, Options, AllowOverride). For a request, Apache first picks the VirtualHosts of the address and port, then the one whose name matches the Host header, otherwise the first one read. With AllowOverride (All, FileInfo…), Apache reads the directories' .htaccess files on every visit: nothing to reload, but every directive must be allowed and its module loaded. Service errors: /var/log/apache2/error.log (or the site's ErrorLog).
    .synopsis = apache2ctl configtest ; apache2ctl -S ; apache2ctl -M ; apache2ctl start|stop|restart|graceful ; apache2ctl -v
    .option-t = checks the configuration (like configtest): "Syntax OK", or the file and line of the error.
    .option-S = the virtual hosts, by address and port: which one answers by default, which name, which file.
    .option-M = the loaded modules.
    .option-v = the version.
    .option-V = the version and build options.
    .example-1 = check the configuration before reloading
    .example-2 = who answers which address, under which name
    .example-3 = the loaded modules (rewrite_module?)
    .example-4 = reload without cutting visits in progress
man-apachectl = the same script as apache2ctl (see man apache2ctl).
    .synopsis = apachectl configtest ; apachectl -S ; apachectl -M ; apachectl start|stop|restart|graceful
    .option-t = checks the configuration.
    .option-S = the virtual hosts, by address and port.
    .option-M = the loaded modules.
    .option-v = the version.
    .option-V = the version and build options.
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = the virtual hosts
man-aide = lists the commands you know.
    .synopsis = help
man-apropos = searches for a word in the summary of every command. Same as man -k.
    .synopsis = apropos word
man-apt = installs and updates software on Debian, as packages downloaded from the project's repositories. apt update downloads the list of available packages: on a brand-new server it is empty, and apt finds nothing until you have run it; it also tells you how many updates are waiting. apt install installs a package and the ones it needs; a package that provides a service (nginx…) starts and enables it right away. apt list --upgradable shows the updates, apt upgrade installs them (full-upgrade may also remove some packages). A new kernel, or a new libc, only takes effect after a reboot: /var/run/reboot-required says so, and /var/run/reboot-required.pkgs says why. An interrupted upgrade (a dropped connection) leaves dpkg half done: you need dpkg --configure -a. On a shaky connection, run it inside tmux. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = apt update ; apt install [-y] package... ; apt list --installed|--upgradable ; apt upgrade [-y]
    .example-1 = fetch the list of available packages (do this first)
    .example-2 = install the nginx web server
    .example-3 = the packages already installed
    .example-4 = the updates that are waiting
    .example-5 = install the updates
    .example-6 = the packages that require a reboot
man-dpkg = the low-level tool that installs .deb packages (apt uses it). If an installation was interrupted (a dropped connection, a power cut), some packages stay unpacked but not configured, and apt refuses to go on: dpkg --configure -a finishes configuring them. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = dpkg --configure -a
    .example-1 = finish an interrupted installation
man-caddy = Caddy, a web server that obtains and renews its HTTPS certificates by itself (automatic HTTPS). It is configured in a Caddyfile (on Debian: /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, read by the caddy service): one block per site, which starts with the site's address. A domain name (cycles.example) gives an HTTPS site, with a certificate from Let's Encrypt and the redirection from HTTP to HTTPS; :80 or http://… give an HTTP-only site. In the block, one directive per line: root * DIRECTORY and file_server serve files, reverse_proxy passes to an app, redir sends elsewhere, respond answers with a text. Subcommands: validate checks a configuration, fmt formats it (--overwrite to rewrite the file), adapt translates it to JSON (the native format), reload sends it to the running Caddy (through its API, on localhost:2019), version gives the version. --config FILE names the configuration (otherwise, the Caddyfile of the current directory).
    .synopsis = caddy validate|adapt|reload [--config FILE] ; caddy fmt [--overwrite] [FILE] ; caddy version
    .example-1 = check the service's Caddyfile
    .example-2 = see the Caddyfile formatted
    .example-3 = rewrite the file, formatted
    .example-4 = send the configuration to the running Caddy (like systemctl reload caddy)
    .example-5 = Caddy's version
man-mailq = shows Postfix's queue: the emails that couldn't leave yet, with their ID, size, date, sender, the reason in brackets (the other server's answer) and the recipients. Same as postqueue -p. "Mail queue is empty": nothing is waiting.
    .synopsis = mailq
    .example-1 = the waiting emails, and why
man-opendkim-genkey = creates a DKIM key pair: SELECTOR.private (the private key, for opendkim, never to be shared) and SELECTOR.txt (the TXT record to publish in the DNS zone, under SELECTOR._domainkey.domain, with the public key). -d gives the domain (it only appears in the .txt's comment), -s the selector (default: default), -D the directory (default: the current directory), -b the size in bits (2048). The files belong to whoever runs the command: opendkim must be able to read the private key (sudo -u opendkim, or chown afterwards).
    .synopsis = opendkim-genkey [-b bits] [-d domain] [-D directory] [-s selector] [-v]
    .option-b = the key size, in bits.
    .option-d = the domain.
    .option-D = the directory to write the files to.
    .option-s = the selector (the key's name).
    .option-v = says what it does.
    .option-r = restricts the key to email.
    .example-1 = a "mail" key for gardens.example, created by the opendkim account
    .example-2 = the record to publish
man-opendkim-testkey = checks the DKIM key published in DNS (SELECTOR._domainkey.domain): that it exists, and that it matches the private key (-k, or KeyFile in /etc/opendkim.conf). Without -v, says nothing when all is well. "key not secure" only means the DNS answer isn't protected by DNSSEC: it isn't an error. It asks the machine's DNS server: also check what the internet sees (dig @… on another server).
    .synopsis = opendkim-testkey -d domain -s selector [-k key] [-vvv]
    .option-d = the domain.
    .option-s = the selector.
    .option-k = the private key to compare.
    .option-v = more details (up to -vvv).
    .example-1 = check gardens.example's "mail" key
man-postconf = shows and changes Postfix's configuration (/etc/postfix/main.cf). With no argument, all parameters; -n only those written in main.cf; -d the default values; NAME, one parameter's value; -x expands $variables. NAME=VALUE (with -e, or without) changes main.cf, then Postfix must be reloaded. A misspelt name is reported: "unused parameter". Some parameters: myhostname, mydestination (the domains received here), myorigin, smtpd_milters (filters for mail received over SMTP), non_smtpd_milters (for mail submitted on the machine), milter_default_action.
    .synopsis = postconf [-n] [-d] [-x] [NAME…] ; postconf -e "NAME = VALUE"
    .option-n = only the parameters in main.cf.
    .option-d = the default values.
    .option-x = expands $variables.
    .option-e = edits main.cf.
    .option-h = the value only, without "NAME =".
    .example-1 = what is set in main.cf
    .example-2 = two parameters
    .example-3 = also sign mail submitted on the machine
man-postfix = controls Postfix, the mail server: check (checks the configuration), reload (rereads it without stopping the service), start, stop, status, flush (retries everything waiting, like postqueue -f). On Debian, you also go through systemctl (reload postfix). Reserved for root, except status.
    .synopsis = postfix check|reload|start|stop|status|flush
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = reread main.cf
    .example-3 = is Postfix running?
man-postqueue = Postfix's queue: -p shows it (like mailq), -f flushes it, i.e. immediately retries sending every waiting message (otherwise Postfix retries by itself, less and less often).
    .synopsis = postqueue -p ; postqueue -f
    .option-p = shows the queue.
    .option-f = retries sending everything waiting.
    .example-1 = what's waiting
    .example-2 = retry now
man-postsuper = maintenance of Postfix's queue: -d ID deletes a message (ALL: all of them; followed by deferred: only the waiting ones). A deleted message will never leave: only for mail that no longer makes sense. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = postsuper -d ID|ALL [deferred]
    .option-d = deletes messages from the queue.
    .example-1 = delete one message
    .example-2 = empty the waiting messages
man-unattended-upgrade = installs updates with nobody in front of the screen (package unattended-upgrades). It reads apt's configuration: 50unattended-upgrades (in /etc/apt/apt.conf.d) says WHAT to install — Origins-Pattern, "key=value,…" patterns that must match a package's origin (origin=Debian, codename=trixie-security, label=Debian-Security…; by default, Debian and its security updates, not third-party repositories), Package-Blacklist (packages never to touch), Mail (who gets the report), Automatic-Reboot and Automatic-Reboot-Time (reboot on its own if a new kernel requires it, and at what time; otherwise right away). 20auto-upgrades says WHEN: the apt-daily-upgrade service, started every morning by its timer, does nothing as long as APT::Periodic::Unattended-Upgrade is "0" (or missing). Its log: /var/log/unattended-upgrades/unattended-upgrades.log. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = unattended-upgrade [--dry-run] [-d | -v]
    .option-d = (--debug) explains each package: its origin, and why it is taken or left.
    .option-v = (--verbose) shows what it does.
    .option-h = (--help) the help.
    .example-1 = see what WOULD be installed, without installing anything, with the reasons
    .example-2 = run a pass right now, showing what happens
    .example-3 = what happened during the automatic passes
man-apt-config = reads apt's configuration as apt understands it: all the files in /etc/apt/apt.conf.d, in alphabetical order, then /etc/apt/apt.conf (a value read later replaces the previous one). apt-config dump shows everything; followed by the start of a key, only what starts that way. A syntax error (a missing ";") is reported with the file and the line.
    .synopsis = apt-config dump [key]
    .example-1 = the whole configuration
    .example-2 = what controls the daily tasks (20auto-upgrades)
    .example-3 = the unattended-upgrades configuration
man-apt_conf = apt's configuration: files in /etc/apt/apt.conf.d, read in the alphabetical order of their names (hence the numbers: 20auto-upgrades, 50unattended-upgrades). A line is written Key::Subkey "value"; — the semicolon is mandatory. A list is written between braces: Key {"{"} "a"; "b"; {"}"};. Comments: // or /* … */. A value read later replaces the previous one; a list, however, grows. apt-config dump shows the result.
    .synopsis = /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/*
    .example-1 = apt's configuration files
    .example-2 = turn on automatic updates
    .example-3 = check what apt understood
man-iostat = shows disk activity (package sysstat). Without an interval, one report; iostat 1 3 gives three, one second apart (the first one sums up what happened since boot). At the top, CPU usage, including %iowait: the time spent waiting for the disk. Then one line per disk: with -x, r/s and w/s (reads and writes per second), rkB/s and wkB/s (throughput), r_await and w_await (the average wait of a request, in ms: a few ms on a healthy disk, hundreds on a saturated one), aqu-sz (the queue) and %util (the share of time the disk is busy: near 100%, it is saturated).
    .synopsis = iostat [-c | -d] [-x] [-k | -m] [-y] [interval [count]]
    .option-c = only the CPU.
    .option-d = only the disks.
    .option-x = detailed statistics (wait, queue, %util).
    .option-k = throughput in kilobytes (default).
    .option-m = throughput in megabytes.
    .option-y = without the first report (the one since boot).
    .option-z = without idle disks.
    .option-h = the help.
    .example-1 = the CPU and the disks, once
    .example-2 = disk details, every second, three times
    .example-3 = only the disks, in MB/s
man-pidstat = shows the activity of each process (package sysstat). -d: its disk I/O (kB_rd/s, kB_wr/s, and iodelay, the time spent waiting for the disk); -u: its CPU usage (%wait: the time spent waiting). With an interval and a count, several measurements, then their average (Average).
    .synopsis = pidstat [-d | -u] [interval [count]]
    .option-d = the disk I/O of each process.
    .option-u = CPU usage (default).
    .option-h = the help.
    .example-1 = who is reading and writing to the disk right now
    .example-2 = the same thing, for five seconds, then the average
man-ionice = gets or sets the I/O priority of a process (util-linux). Three classes: 1, realtime (root only); 2, best-effort (the normal class, with a priority from 0, the highest, to 7); 3, idle (the process only gets the disk when nobody else asks for it). Beware: these priorities only matter if the disk's scheduler supports them (bfq, mq-deadline: see /sys/block/DISK/queue/scheduler); "none", common on virtual disks and NVMe SSDs, ignores them. To limit a service no matter what: systemd's IOWriteBandwidthMax=.
    .synopsis = ionice -p PID ; ionice -c class [-n prio] -p PID
    .option-c = the class: 1 (realtime), 2 (best-effort), 3 (idle).
    .option-n = the priority within the class, from 0 (the highest) to 7.
    .option-p = the target process (its PID).
    .option-h = the help.
    .example-1 = the priority of a process
    .example-2 = make it idle
    .example-3 = the disk's scheduler, which decides whether ionice matters
man-tmux = a terminal multiplexer: sessions that live on the machine, independently of your connection. If the connection drops, whatever runs in tmux keeps going; you reattach to it afterwards. tmux new -s name opens a session (its name shows before the prompt); tmux detach leaves it while keeping it running (on a real keyboard: Ctrl+b then d); tmux ls lists the sessions; tmux attach -t name reattaches to one; exit, inside the session, closes it. For a long operation on a server (upgrade, copy, migration), always run it in tmux.
    .synopsis = tmux new [-s name] ; tmux ls ; tmux attach [-t name] ; tmux detach ; tmux kill-session -t name
    .example-1 = open a session named upgrade
    .example-2 = leave it while keeping it running
    .example-3 = the running sessions
    .example-4 = reattach to the upgrade session (after a disconnection, for example)
    .example-5 = close the upgrade session
man-uname = prints information about the system: -r the version of the kernel that is RUNNING (not necessarily the installed one: a new kernel only starts at reboot), -a everything.
    .synopsis = uname [-r] [-a]
    .option-r = the version of the running kernel (release).
    .option-a = everything: system, machine name, kernel, architecture.
    .example-1 = the version of the running kernel
    .example-2 = everything
man-bash = your session's shell (Bourne-Again SHell): it reads the commands you type. bash script runs a script, like sh.
    .synopsis = bash script [argument...]
    .example-1 = run a script
man-certbot = requests and renews free HTTPS certificates from Let's Encrypt. To prove you control the site, certbot drops a "challenge" file that the authority comes to fetch over HTTP: with --webroot, in the directory given by -w, which must be the one the web server serves for that name; with --nginx (package python3-certbot-nginx), nginx serves it. Each certificate is valid for 90 days; a systemd timer runs certbot renew twice a day, which renews whatever expires in less than 30 days. How to renew is recorded in /etc/letsencrypt/renewal/name.conf (rewritten at each successful renewal). A deploy hook is a command run after each successful renewal, for example to reload nginx; so are the executable scripts in /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = certbot certificates ; certbot renew [--dry-run] [--webroot -w directory] [--deploy-hook command] ; certbot certonly --webroot -w directory -d name [-d name...] ; certbot reconfigure --cert-name name [options]
    .example-1 = the certificates, their names and their expiry dates
    .example-2 = dress rehearsal: the whole renewal, without changing anything
    .example-3 = renew by dropping the challenge in the site's directory
    .example-4 = reload nginx after each successful renewal
    .example-5 = change how renewal is done, tested without renewing now
man-createdb = creates an empty PostgreSQL database. You need the right to do so: sudo -u postgres createdb name.
    .synopsis = createdb name
    .example-1 = a test database, to try out a restore
man-date = prints the machine's date and time. With +FORMAT, writes them your way: %Y the year, %m the month, %d the day, %H the hour, %M the minutes, %S the seconds, %F for 2027-05-17, %T for 20:30:12. Handy for naming a directory or a backup: date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S. %Z and %z give the time zone (CEST, +0200). The time shown is in the machine's time zone (timedatectl). -s sets the clock by hand (root): best avoided, NTP does it better.
    .synopsis = date [+FORMAT] ; date -s "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS"
    .example-1 = the date and time
    .example-2 = a name that sorts in order: year, month, day, hour, minutes, seconds
    .example-3 = the date alone, in ISO format
    .example-4 = the time, with the time zone's offset from UTC
man-dropdb = deletes a PostgreSQL database and everything in it, without confirmation. Keep it for test databases.
    .synopsis = dropdb name
    .example-1 = delete the test database
man-logrotate = rotates logs so they do not grow forever: every day (or week…), the log is renamed (.1), the older ones move up a notch (.2, .3…), the oldest is deleted, and old ones are compressed (.gz). Run every day by cron or a systemd timer; its configuration: /etc/logrotate.conf, and one file per program in /etc/logrotate.d/. One block per log pattern, between braces: daily, weekly; rotate 7 (how many to keep); compress, delaycompress; missingok (no error if it is missing); notifempty (not if it is empty); create 0640 radio adm (the new file) or copytruncate (copy then empty, for a program that cannot reopen its log); postrotate … endscript (a command afterwards, for example systemctl reload). Beware: a program keeps its file open; once renamed, it keeps writing into it, hence copytruncate or postrotate. -d tries the configuration without doing anything; -f forces the rotation.
    .synopsis = logrotate [-d] [-f] [-v] configuration_file
    .example-1 = try a configuration, without touching anything
    .example-2 = force a rotation now
    .example-3 = an example configuration shipped with Debian
man-lsblk = lists disks and their partitions (list block devices): their name (/dev/sdb1…), their size, whether they are removable (RM = 1, a USB stick or disk) and where they are mounted. -f adds the file system and the label (LABEL), handy for recognizing a disk.
    .synopsis = lsblk [-f] [-d] [-o COLUMNS]
    .option-f = shows the file system, the label and the UUID (filesystems).
    .option-o = chooses the columns, separated by commas: NAME, SIZE, TYPE, MOUNTPOINTS, MODEL (the disk model), SERIAL (its serial number), FSTYPE, UUID…
    .option-d = only the disks, without their partitions (nodeps).
    .example-1 = the disks and their partitions
    .example-2 = with their labels
    .example-3 = each disk, its size, its model and its serial number
man-smartctl = asks a disk about its health (SMART, the monitoring built into the disk; smartmontools package). -H gives the overall verdict: PASSED only means that no attribute has crossed the manufacturer's threshold, not that the disk is fine. The attributes (-A) tell what matters: Reallocated_Sector_Ct (5), damaged sectors the disk has replaced with spare ones; Current_Pending_Sector (197), sectors it can no longer read and is waiting to replace; Offline_Uncorrectable (198); UDMA_CRC_Error_Count (199), cable errors rather than disk errors. A count that keeps growing announces a failure: replace the disk before it dies. -t short runs a short test (two minutes), whose result shows in -l selftest. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = smartctl [-a | -H | -i | -A] [-l error|selftest] [-t short|long] /dev/DISK
    .option-a = everything: identity, verdict, attributes, error and test logs.
    .option-H = the overall verdict (health).
    .option-i = the identity of the disk: model, serial number, capacity.
    .option-A = the SMART attributes, with their value, threshold and raw value (RAW_VALUE).
    .option-l = a log: error (the errors the disk saw) or selftest (the test results).
    .option-t = runs a test: short (a few minutes) or long (several hours).
    .option-x = even more than -a (here, the same thing).
    .example-1 = the overall verdict for a disk
    .example-2 = everything about a disk
    .example-3 = run a short test
    .example-4 = the test results
man-mdadm = manages Linux software RAID (md): arrays of disks, or of partitions, that act as one. In RAID 1, each member keeps a full copy: if one dies, the array carries on with the others (it is "degraded"). cat /proc/mdstat shows the state of every array: [UU], both members are fine; [U_], one is missing; (F), a faulty member; and the rebuild in progress (recovery). To replace a disk: mark its member faulty (--fail), remove it (--remove), then, once the new disk is partitioned, add the new member (--add): the array rebuilds it by copying the others. A disk often holds several partitions, each in its own array: remove them from each one. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = mdadm --detail /dev/mdN ; mdadm [--manage] /dev/mdN --fail PARTITION --remove PARTITION ; mdadm [--manage] /dev/mdN --add PARTITION
    .option-D = the detailed state of an array (--detail): its members, their role, the rebuild.
    .option-f = marks a member faulty (--fail): the array stops using it.
    .option-r = removes a faulty member from the array (--remove); "failed" removes them all.
    .option-a = adds a member (--add): if a member is missing, it takes its place and is rebuilt; --re-add puts back a former member.
    .option-C = creates an array (--create /dev/mdN --level=1 --raid-devices=2 DISKS); mdadm asks for confirmation (--run to skip it). The array is usable right away, during its first synchronization (resync). "missing" instead of a disk leaves its slot empty: the array is born degraded, to be completed later with --add.
    .example-1 = the state of every array
    .example-2 = the details of an array
    .example-3 = take a member out of the md0 array
    .example-4 = add a new member (it gets rebuilt)
    .example-5 = create a RAID 1 array on two new disks
    .example-6 = its ARRAY line, to add to /etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf
man-pvs = lists LVM physical volumes (PV): the disks, partitions or RAID arrays LVM uses, their group (VG), their size and their free space (PFree). LVM writes sizes in units of 1024: 1.79t, and "<" when the rounded figure is larger than the real size. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = pvs
    .example-1 = the physical volumes
man-vgs = lists LVM volume groups (VG): how many PVs (#PV) and logical volumes (#LV) they hold, their size (VSize) and the space left to hand out (VFree). Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = vgs
    .example-1 = the volume groups
man-lvs = lists LVM logical volumes (LV): the ones carrying file systems, their group and their size (LSize). The file system can be smaller than its LV: df shows the former, lvs the latter. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = lvs
    .example-1 = the logical volumes
man-pvcreate = prepares a device (disk, partition or RAID array) for LVM: it becomes a physical volume (PV), which you then add to a group (vgextend). It refuses a partitioned disk, or one that is already part of a RAID array. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = pvcreate DEVICE…
    .example-1 = make the md2 array a physical volume
man-vgextend = adds one or more physical volumes to a volume group: its free space (VFree) grows accordingly. A device that is not a PV yet is prepared along the way. Careful: a group is no safer than its least safe PV; a lone disk, without RAID, added to a RAID group loses everything if it dies. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = vgextend GROUP DEVICE…
    .example-1 = add md2 to the labo group
man-vgreduce = removes a physical volume from a group. It refuses while a logical volume sits on it ("still in use"): pvmove first moves its data away. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = vgreduce GROUP DEVICE…
    .example-1 = take sdc out of the labo group
man-pvmove = moves a physical volume's data to the other PVs of its group (or to the ones you name), while everything keeps running. There must be enough free space elsewhere. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = pvmove SOURCE [DESTINATION…]
    .example-1 = empty sdc into md2
man-pvremove = wipes the LVM label from a device: it stops being a physical volume and becomes free again (for a RAID array, for example). It refuses a PV still in a group: vgreduce first. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = pvremove DEVICE…
    .example-1 = make sdc free again
man-lvextend = grows a logical volume: -L gives a size (+500G: 500 GiB more; 2T: 2 TiB in total), -l extents (+100%FREE: all the group's free space). The file system does not follow on its own: df does not change until you grow it too (resize2fs), except with -r, which does it right away. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = lvextend [-r] (-L [+]SIZE | -l [+]EXTENTS[%FREE]) VG/LV [PV…]
    .option-L = the new size, or what to add with + (units k, m, g, t).
    .option-l = the size in 4 MiB extents, or as a share of the free space (+100%FREE).
    .option-r = also grows the file system (resizefs).
    .example-1 = 500 GiB more for the photos volume
    .example-2 = all the free space, file system included
man-resize2fs = grows (or shrinks) an ext2, ext3 or ext4 file system; without a size, it takes all the space of its device (after an lvextend, for example). A mounted ext4 grows without unmounting (on-line resizing); to shrink it, you must unmount it. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = resize2fs DEVICE [SIZE]
    .example-1 = take all the space of the logical volume
    .example-2 = check the new size
man-update-initramfs = rebuilds the initramfs: the small system the kernel loads at boot, before the disks. It carries a copy of /etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf: after creating an array or changing that file, rebuild it, or the array comes up under another name (md127) or not at all. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = update-initramfs -u
    .option-u = updates the initramfs of the running kernel.
    .example-1 = rebuild the initramfs after changing mdadm.conf
man-sfdisk = reads and writes partition tables (util-linux), without dialogue: through a script. -l shows them; -d writes a disk's table as a script, which sfdisk can read back to copy it onto another disk (sfdisk -d /dev/sda | sfdisk /dev/sdb: the new disk is then split exactly like the other one). Mind the direction: the disk named last is the one overwritten. sfdisk refuses to touch a disk in use (a mounted partition, or one in a RAID array). Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = sfdisk -l [DISK] ; sfdisk -d DISK ; sfdisk DISK < script
    .option-d = writes the disk's table as a script (dump).
    .option-l = shows the partitions (list).
    .example-1 = the partitions of a disk
    .example-2 = its table, as a script
    .example-3 = copy sda's table onto sdb (the new disk)
man-grub-install = installs GRUB, the boot loader, on a disk: it is what the computer runs first. On a RAID 1 server, install it on each disk: if the one that boots dies, the other must be able to boot alone. On a GPT table booted by the BIOS, GRUB needs its small "BIOS boot" partition. Aim at the whole disk (/dev/sdb), not a partition. Reserved for root.
    .synopsis = grub-install /dev/DISK
    .example-1 = make the sdb disk able to boot
man-lsof = lists open files, and the processes holding them (list open files). +L1 only shows files that were deleted but are still open: their space is only freed when they are closed (when the program stops or reopens its file). It is the classic cause of a full df while du finds nothing. Without being root, you only see your own processes.
    .synopsis = lsof [+L1] [-p pid] [-u account] [file]
    .example-1 = files deleted but still open
    .example-2 = who is holding this log open?
    .example-3 = the files opened by a process
man-mount = attaches a file system (a partition, a USB stick, a network share) to the tree, at a mount point: an existing directory, whose visible content becomes that of the device. Without arguments, lists what is mounted. -o ro mounts read-only: nothing can be written or deleted there (the right way to plug in a backup you only want to read). Reserved for root. umount detaches.
    .synopsis = mount [-o ro] device mount_point ; mount mount_point ; mount -a
    .example-1 = what is mounted
    .example-2 = attach the sdb1 partition read-only on /mnt/usb
    .example-3 = detach it
    .example-4 = mount everything /etc/fstab describes (except noauto): the right way to test that file before rebooting
    .command-2 = sudo mount -o ro /dev/sdb1 /mnt/usb
    .command-3 = sudo umount /mnt/usb
man-fstab = /etc/fstab describes the file systems to mount at boot, one line each: the source, the mount point, the type (ext4…), the options (defaults, ro, nofail, noauto…), dump (0) and the fsck check order (pass: 1 for /, 2 for the others, 0 for none). Give the source preferably as UUID=… or LABEL=… (blkid or lsblk -f show them): names like /dev/sdc1 depend on the order the disks are found in, and change when you add one. nofail: if the source is missing, the boot carries on (essential for a USB disk). At boot, systemd waits 90 seconds for each source; a missing source without nofail stops the machine in emergency mode. After changing fstab: findmnt --verify, mount -a, then systemctl daemon-reload (systemd derives its mount units from this file).
    .synopsis = SOURCE  MOUNT_POINT  TYPE  OPTIONS  DUMP  PASS
    .example-1 = read it
    .example-2 = check it
    .example-3 = mount what it describes, to try it without rebooting
man-blkid = shows device identifiers: their UUID, their label (LABEL) and their type (ext4, linux_raid_member, LVM2_member…). That is what belongs in /etc/fstab, rather than /dev/sdX, which can change. -U and -L find the device for a UUID or a label.
    .synopsis = blkid [device…] ; blkid -U uuid ; blkid -L label
    .option-U = the device carrying this UUID.
    .option-L = the device carrying this label.
    .example-1 = every device and its identifiers
    .example-2 = those of one partition
    .example-3 = which device carries the SCANS label?
man-findmnt = shows what is mounted, as a tree (TARGET, SOURCE, FSTYPE, OPTIONS). findmnt --verify rereads /etc/fstab and reports what would get stuck at boot: a source that can't be found, a type that doesn't match, a missing mount point. Run it after every change to fstab, before rebooting.
    .synopsis = findmnt [mount_point] ; findmnt --verify [--verbose]
    .example-1 = everything that is mounted
    .example-2 = what is mounted on /srv
    .example-3 = check /etc/fstab before rebooting
man-openssl = the cryptography toolbox (TLS, certificates, keys). openssl x509 reads a certificate: its dates (-dates), its holder (-subject), its issuer (-issuer), its names (-ext subjectAltName); -checkend seconds tells whether it expires within that time. openssl s_client connects to a server over TLS and shows the certificate it really presents, and whether it can be trusted (Verify return code).
    .synopsis = openssl x509 [-in file] -noout [-dates] [-subject] [-issuer] [-checkend seconds] ; openssl s_client -connect host:port [-servername name]
    .example-1 = the validity dates of a certificate
    .example-2 = the certificate the server really presents, and its dates
    .example-3 = does it expire within fifteen days (1,296,000 seconds)?
man-awk = a small language for reading lines split into fields. Each line is cut at spaces: $1 is the first field, $2 the second, $NF the last, $0 the whole line; NR is the line number. A program is a series of "pattern {"{"} action {"}"}": the action applies to the lines where the pattern is true (no pattern: all of them; no action: the line is printed). BEGIN runs before the first line, END after the last. Arrays are great for counting: c[$1]++ then, in END, for (k in c) print c[k], k. Put the program between single quotes, so the shell leaves the $ alone.
    .synopsis = awk [-F separator] [-v variable=value] 'program' [file...]
    .example-1 = the first field of each line (the address, in a web log)
    .example-2 = the lines whose 9th field (the HTTP code) is 502
    .example-3 = count the HTTP codes
    .example-4 = add up the 10th field (the bytes sent)
    .example-5 = with another separator (the accounts in /etc/passwd)
man-cat = prints the content of files (concatenate: it can join several end to end). Without a file, copies whatever comes in through a pipe.
    .synopsis = cat file...
    .example-1 = print a file
man-cd = changes place (change directory). "cd .." goes up one level, "cd ~" or plain "cd" takes you back home, "cd /" to the root, "cd -" to the previous place.
    .synopsis = cd place
    .example-1 = enter the place Port
    .example-2 = go up one level
    .example-3 = go back home
    .example-4 = go to the root
    .example-5 = go back to the previous place (and print it)
man-chmod = changes permissions (change mode). E.g.: chmod o-w place takes away from others (o) the right to write (w); chmod 755 place gives rwx to the owner, r-x to others. Only the owner (or root) can do it. -R also acts on the whole content of a directory. g+s sets the setgid bit on a directory (an s instead of the group's x in ls -l): what is created inside gets the directory's group, not that of the person creating it; in octal, it is the leading 2 (2775). +t (sticky, the leading 1): in a shared directory, everyone can only delete their own files.
    .synopsis = chmod [-R] mode file
    .option-R = acts on the directory and on everything it contains (recursive).
    .example-1 = take away the others' right to write
    .example-2 = rwx for the owner, r-x for others
    .example-3 = give the group the right to write, across a whole directory
    .example-4 = a shared directory: what is created inside keeps the directory's group
    .command-1 = chmod o-w memories
    .command-2 = chmod 755 place
    .command-3 = sudo chmod -R g+w /srv/team
    .command-4 = sudo chmod g+s /srv/team
man-chage = shows and changes an account's dates (change age): -l shows them, -E sets the account's expiry date (a date YYYY-MM-DD; 0: already expired; -1: never). An expired account can no longer log in, neither with a password nor with an SSH key. Reserved for root (except -l for your own account).
    .synopsis = chage -l account ; chage -E date account
    .option-l = shows the account's dates (list).
    .option-E = sets the account's expiry date (expire date).
    .example-1 = the dates of marc's account
    .example-2 = marc's account has expired: no more logins
    .example-3 = the account no longer expires
man-clear = clears the screen.
    .synopsis = clear
man-cp = copies a file (copy). The original stays in place. -r copies a whole place with all its content.
    .synopsis = cp [-r] source destination
    .option-r = copies a whole place, with everything it contains (recursive).
    .example-1 = make a copy next to it
    .example-2 = copy a file from elsewhere to here (.)
man-curl = fetches a web page, like a browser but without displaying it nicely. -I only asks for the response header (the status code, for example 200 = OK). Over HTTPS (https://…), curl checks the server's certificate like a browser: validity dates, site name, trusted authority; if there is a problem, it stops with error 60.
    .synopsis = curl [-I] [-i] [-k] address
    .option-I = only asks for the response header (HEAD).
    .option-i = shows the response header, then the page (include).
    .option-k = does not check the certificate (insecure): only for a test, never for real.
man-cut = splits each line into fields according to the separator and keeps field N (or N,M). E.g.: cut -d " " -f 1 keeps the first word.
    .synopsis = cut -d separator -f N [file]
man-dmesg = prints the kernel messages (display message): the hardware detected at boot, disk errors… and the OOM killer ("Out of memory: Killed process"), when the kernel kills a process for lack of memory. Each line starts with the time elapsed since boot, in seconds; -T turns it into a date and time. Reserved for root on Debian; journalctl -k shows the same messages.
    .synopsis = dmesg [-T]
    .option-T = readable date and time, instead of seconds since boot.
    .example-1 = the kernel messages
    .example-2 = with the date and time
    .example-3 = only those from the OOM killer
man-df = free space on storage (disk free): disk, USB stick… -h writes in K, M, G. -i counts inodes instead of bytes: each file (and each place) uses an inode, and their number is fixed when the file system is created. So storage can be "full" (No space left on device) with free space left, if it has no inode left.
    .synopsis = df [-h] [-i] [place]
    .option-h = readable sizes: K, M, G.
    .option-i = the inodes (IUsed: used, IFree: free) instead of bytes.
    .example-1 = the space on each storage device
    .example-2 = the inodes on each storage device
    .example-3 = the inodes of one storage device, in readable numbers
man-dig = asks the DNS a question: "what is the IP address of this name?" (domain information groper). @server chooses the DNS server to query (otherwise, the one in /etc/resolv.conf); you can ask for another record type than A (IPv4 address): AAAA (IPv6), CNAME (alias), MX (mail), NS (the zone's servers), TXT, SOA (including the serial number). +short keeps only the answer. In the answer, the number before IN is the TTL: how many seconds a resolver may keep this answer in its cache. The aa flag (authoritative answer) says it comes from a server that is authoritative for the zone.
    .synopsis = dig [@server] name [type] [+short]
    .example-1 = the address of a name, asked of the usual resolver
    .example-2 = the same question, asked directly of an authoritative server
    .example-3 = the DNS servers of a zone
    .example-4 = the SOA: its serial number changes with every modification
man-docker = runs applications in containers: each with its own files, isolated from the rest of the machine, from an image (a ready-to-use application, downloaded with pull). docker run creates and starts a container: -d in the background, --name to name it, -p host:container publishes a port, -v name:/path mounts a volume into it (or -v /directory:/path a directory of the machine), -e VARIABLE=value, --restart unless-stopped so it comes back with the machine, --user uid:gid (or -u) to run it under another account than the one the image plans for: without USER in the image nor --user, the container runs as root. A container's uid is the same as on the machine (root inside is root on the mounted directories); a name (--user node) must exist in the image's /etc/passwd. --read-only forbids writing anywhere but in volumes, --cap-drop ALL removes root's special privileges, --security-opt no-new-privileges prevents regaining any. Beware: whatever a container writes outside a volume lives in its own layer, and disappears with it (docker rm); updating an application means deleting the container and creating a new one with the new image. docker ps (-a) lists containers, logs shows their log, exec runs a command inside, cp copies files between the machine and a container. You must be root (or in the docker group, which amounts to the same thing).
    .synopsis = docker run [-d] [--name name] [-p port:port] [-v volume:/path] [-e VAR=value] [--restart policy] [--user uid:gid] [--read-only] [--cap-drop ALL] [--security-opt no-new-privileges] image ; docker ps [-a] ; docker logs container ; docker exec [-u user] container command ; docker cp source destination ; docker stop|start|rm container ; docker pull image ; docker images ; docker volume ls|create|rm ; docker inspect container
    .example-1 = the containers, even stopped ones
    .example-2 = a container's log
    .example-3 = what is in a directory of the container
    .example-4 = copy a directory from the container to the machine
    .example-5 = a container in the background, its port published, its data in a volume, and which comes back with the machine
    .example-6 = a container's volumes
    .example-7 = which account does the container run as?
    .example-8 = a container that does not run as root
man-docker-compose = runs several containers that go together (an app and its database, for example), described in the compose.yaml file of the current directory: under services:, one block per service, with image:, ports:, volumes:, environment:, depends_on: (start first), restart: and user: (the account, like docker run --user; for example user: "1001:1001", in quotes). Compose creates a network for the project: each service can be reached on it by its name (db, appli). Named volumes are declared under volumes: at the bottom of the file. ${"{"}VARIABLE{"}"} is replaced by the value from the directory's .env file (handy to keep secrets out of the file). You type docker compose (with a space): it is a docker subcommand.
    .synopsis = docker compose up [-d] ; docker compose down [-v] ; docker compose ps ; docker compose logs [service] ; docker compose config ; docker compose restart [service] ; docker compose exec service command
    .example-1 = create and start (or recreate) the services, in the background
    .example-2 = the state of the services
    .example-3 = a service's log
    .example-4 = the file as Compose understands it, variables replaced
    .example-5 = stop and delete everything (with -v, the volumes too: their data is lost)
man-diff = compares two files line by line, and shows what to change to go from the first to the second. In normal format, "3c3" means: line 3 of the first becomes line 3 of the second (c for change, d for delete, a for add); lines starting with < come from the first file, those with > from the second. -u gives the unified format, the one of git diff: - for what goes, + for what comes, with three lines around. With two places, diff compares the files with the same name, and -r goes down into sub-places. Nothing is printed if the files are identical. --color shows what goes in red and what comes in green (on screen only; --color=always even in a pipe).
    .synopsis = diff [-u] [-r] [-q] [--color[=WHEN]] file1 file2
    .option-u = unified format (unified): - and +, with three lines of context.
    .option-r = also compares sub-places, recursively.
    .option-q = only says whether the files differ (quiet).
    .example-1 = what changes between two files
    .example-2 = the same thing, in git diff format
    .example-3 = everything that differs between two places
man-du = measures the space used (disk usage), place by place. A file with several names (hard links) is only counted once. -s gives only the total, -h writes in K, M, G, -d N stops N levels deep. --inodes counts files (inodes) instead of bytes: to find the place that holds thousands of files. E.g.: du -sh *
    .synopsis = du [-s] [-h] [-d N] [--inodes] [place...]
    .option-h = readable sizes: K, M, G.
    .option-s = a single total per place (summary), without the details.
    .option-d = only show N levels below the given place (--max-depth=N).
    .example-1 = the space taken by each log
    .example-2 = the number of files, place by place, one level deep
    .example-3 = the number of files in each place
man-echo = prints the text. Very useful with > to write into a file: echo "hello" > note.txt
    .synopsis = echo text
man-exit = leaves the shell. In an ssh session, closes the connection and takes you back to the machine you came from (here, your workstation).
    .synopsis = exit
man-find = searches for files in a place and all its sub-places. E.g.: find . -name "*.pdf"; -type f keeps only files, -type d only directories; -mtime +30 those modified more than 30 days ago (-mtime -7: less than 7 days); -maxdepth 1 does not go down into sub-places; -delete deletes what is found (careful: run it first without -delete to see the list). find walks through places by itself: it deletes hundreds of thousands of files where rm * fails ("Argument list too long").
    .synopsis = find [place] [-maxdepth N] [-name pattern] [-type f|d] [-mtime [+|-]N] [-delete]
    .example-1 = the text files, here and below
    .example-2 = the statistics older than 30 days (to look)
    .example-3 = delete them
man-free = shows the machine's RAM (and swap): total, used, free, the disk cache (buff/cache) and above all available, what is really left for starting programs. -h writes in Ki, Mi, Gi; -m in megabytes. To find out WHO is taking the memory: top or ps aux.
    .synopsis = free [-h] [-m]
    .option-h = readable sizes: Ki, Mi, Gi.
    .option-m = in megabytes (MiB).
    .option-b = in bytes.
    .option-k = in kilobytes (default).
    .option-g = in gigabytes.
    .example-1 = the memory, in readable sizes
man-git =
    keeps the history of a directory (a "repository"). The main commands:
      git init                    turns the place into a repository
      git status                  what has changed since the last commit
      git add file (or .)         stages a file for the next commit
      git commit -m "message"     records a snapshot of all the staged files
      git commit -am "message"    the same, first staging all modified tracked files
      git rm file                 deletes a file and stops tracking it
      git log [--oneline]         the history of commits
      git diff [ID] [file]        the differences (- before, + after)
      git show [ID]               a commit and its changes
      git restore --source=ID f   puts file f back as it was in commit ID
    To work with others, or to publish, you send your commits to another repository (a "remote"):
      git init --bare place.git   creates a bare repository (no working copy), like on a server
      git clone place.git         copies a repository (its remote is called origin)
      git remote add name place   declares a remote; git remote -v lists them
      git push name main          sends your commits to the remote
      git pull name main          fetches the remote's commits
    A bare repository can react to a push: its hooks (hooks/pre-receive, hooks/post-receive) are scripts run on each reception, if they are executable (chmod +x). pre-receive can refuse the push (non-zero exit code). To deploy, post-receive checks the files out into a place: git --work-tree=place checkout -f main.
    On screen, status, diff, log and show are in colour (color.ui=auto): what goes in red, what comes in green; --no-color turns them off.
    A commit is designated by the start of its identifier (at least 4 characters), or by HEAD (the latest), HEAD~1 (the one before)…
    .synopsis = git [--git-dir=repository] [--work-tree=place] command
man-grep = prints the lines that contain the pattern. The pattern is a regular expression: . means "any character", ^ the start of the line, $ the end, [abc] one character among a, b, c, and * "as many times as you like" whatever precedes it; to search for an actual dot, write \. (in single quotes). -E also allows + ? | and parentheses (extended expressions), -F searches for the text as is. -i ignores case, -v keeps the lines that do NOT contain it, -c counts the lines, -q prints nothing (only the exit code matters: success if the pattern is found, useful in a script). Without a file, searches what comes in through a pipe. "grep word *" searches all the files in the place. On screen, what matches is highlighted in red (--color=auto, like Debian's alias); --color=never turns it off.
    .synopsis = grep [-i] [-v] [-c] [-q] [-E|-F] [--color[=WHEN]] pattern [file...]
    .option-i = ignores the difference between uppercase and lowercase.
    .option-v = inverts the search: keeps the lines that do NOT contain the pattern.
    .option-c = counts the matching lines instead of printing them.
    .option-q = silent (quiet): prints nothing, succeeds if the pattern is found, fails otherwise.
    .option-E = extended regular expressions: + ? | ( ) have their special meaning.
    .option-F = searches for the pattern as is, without regular expressions (fixed strings).
    .example-1 = the lines that mention the Eraser
    .example-2 = in all the files here, uppercase or not
    .example-3 = a pattern with spaces, in quotes
    .example-4 = the lines that contain ".env" (the dot escaped)
    .example-5 = the GET or POST requests to the till app
    .command-1 = grep Eraser chronicle.txt
    .command-2 = grep -i eraser *
    .command-3 = grep "my password" journal.txt
man-head = prints the beginning of a file (the first 10 lines, or N).
    .synopsis = head [-n N] [file]
    .option-n = number of lines to print (10 by default): head -n 3 file.
man-kill = asks a process to stop (it receives a signal). PID is its number, given by ps. kill -9 PID forces it to stop. You can only stop your own processes (or all of them, as root). A zombie (<defunct>) reacts to no signal: it is already dead, only its parent can reap it.
    .synopsis = kill PID
man-last = lists the latest logins to the machine (from /var/log/wtmp): who, from which address, when, and for how long (still logged in: still connected). With an account name, only its logins; -n N, only the last N. Useful for piecing together a night of incident: who stepped in, and at what time.
    .synopsis = last [-n N] [account…]
    .option-n = only the N most recent logins.
    .example-1 = all recent logins
    .example-2 = those of one account
man-less = shows a file page by page. Here, files are short: less does the same as cat.
    .synopsis = less file
man-ln = creates a link (link). With -s, a symbolic link: a small file that holds the path of another, and that commands follow to read, write or enter (ls -l shows it with an arrow ->). If the destination is a place, the link inside it takes the target's name. Beware: a relative path is read from the link's place, not from where you are; when in doubt, give an absolute path. Deleting the link (rm) does not delete the target file. Without -s, a hard link: a second name for the same file. To replace a link that points to a place (a "current" link to the live version), you need -f and -n: without -n, ln goes into the target place and creates the new link there. Replacing a link is instantaneous: that is how you switch from one version to another, and how you roll back.
    .synopsis = ln [-s] [-f] [-n] [-v] target [link | place]
    .option-s = symbolic link (otherwise: hard link).
    .option-f = replaces the link if it already exists (force).
    .option-n = if the destination is a link to a place, treat it as a file (no-dereference): with -f, the link is replaced.
    .option-v = says what was done (verbose).
    .example-1 = enable an nginx site, the Debian way
    .example-2 = see the links and what they point to
    .example-3 = switch the current link to another version, in one go
man-ls = lists the content of a place (list). -a also shows hidden files, whose name starts with a dot. -l (long) shows the permissions, the owner and the size; -h writes sizes in K, M, G; -n shows the numbers (uid, gid) instead of the names. On screen, names are in colour (--color=auto, like the alias in Debian's ~/.bashrc): places in blue, links in cyan, programs in green; --color=never turns colours off, --color=always keeps them even in a pipe.
    .synopsis = ls [-a] [-l] [-d] [-h] [-n] [--color[=WHEN]] [place]
    .option-a = shows everything (all), including hidden files, whose name starts with a dot, and the places . and ..
    .option-l = long format: type, permissions, owner, group, size and name, one entry per line.
    .option-h = with -l, writes sizes in a readable way (human-readable): 1.2K, 34M, 5G.
    .option-n = like -l, but with the numbers of the owner (uid) and the group (gid) instead of their names (numeric).
    .option-i = shows the inode number of each file: two names with the same number are the same file (a hard link).
    .option-d = shows the place itself, not what it contains (directory): with -l, its own permissions.
    .example-1 = what is here
    .example-2 = everything, even what is hidden
    .example-3 = the permissions of the place memories
    .example-4 = the permissions of the /srv/till directory itself
    .command-3 = ls -l ~/memories
man-mail = sends mail (mailx): the text comes through a pipe (echo … | mail -s subject recipient). -s gives the subject; -E sends nothing if the text is empty (handy after a command that stays silent when all is well). On a server, local mail goes to /var/mail/account; /etc/aliases can forward it elsewhere (root: zoe). With no recipient, mail lists the messages received.
    .synopsis = mail [-E] [-s subject] recipient… ; mail
    .example-1 = send one line to zoe
    .example-2 = send only if the command wrote something
    .example-3 = read your mailbox
    .command-1 = echo "Hello" | mail -s Test zoe
    .command-2 = command | mail -E -s Alert zoe
man-man = displays the manual of a command (manual). It's the first reflex when you no longer remember how a command works: its syntax, its options, examples. On a real Linux, the page opens full screen: you scroll with the arrow keys, search with /word and quit with q.
    .synopsis = man [-k word] command
    .option-k = searches for a word in the summary of every command (like apropos), to find the command that does what you want.
    .example-1 = the manual of ls
    .example-2 = which commands talk about copying?
    .example-3 = a quick summary, without opening the manual
man-mkdir = creates a new place, that is, a directory (make directory). -p also creates the missing parent places, and says nothing if the place already exists.
    .synopsis = mkdir [-p] place
    .option-p = also creates the parent places (parents), with no error if they exist.
    .example-1 = create a place
    .example-2 = create a place and all its parents at once
man-mv = moves a file into a place (move), or renames it if the destination is a new name.
    .synopsis = mv source destination
    .example-1 = move crab into the place Bucket
    .example-2 = rename
    .example-3 = move and rename at once
    .command-1 = mv crab Bucket
    .command-2 = mv draft.txt letter.txt
    .command-3 = mv draft.txt ~/Harbour/letter.txt
man-nc = netcat, the Swiss army knife of networking. Here, to test whether a TCP port on a machine answers, as anyone on the internet would: -z tests without sending anything, -v reports the result. "succeeded": the port is open; "Connection refused": nobody is listening (or the firewall rejects); "timed out": the firewall drops the packets. Testing from ANOTHER machine shows what the world sees; from the machine itself, the firewall does not get involved.
    .synopsis = nc -zv machine port[-port]
    .example-1 = does port 22 (SSH) of the machine workshop answer?
    .example-2 = ports 5430 to 5435
man-nginx = the web server (and proxy). You don't start it by hand: systemd takes care of it (systemctl start, reload nginx). Its configuration starts from /etc/nginx/nginx.conf, which includes the sites in /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/: one server block per site, with listen (the port; default_server for the site that answers when no name matches, only one per port), server_name (the site's names), root (the directory of the pages) and index (the home page). Every directive ends with a semicolon. Before any reload: sudo nginx -t, which gives the file and line of the error. Several sites on one machine (virtual hosts): one server block per site, each with its own server_name. In a location block (a path prefix), proxy_pass hands the visit to another program (reverse proxy): with an address ending in a slash (http://127.0.0.1:3000/), the location prefix is replaced by /, otherwise the path is passed as is. return 301 https://$host$request_uri; redirects elsewhere. Service errors go to /var/log/nginx/error.log. Rate limiting: limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=name:10m rate=2r/s; (in the http block, one key per address, 2 requests per second) then limit_req zone=name burst=10 nodelay; where you want to limit: above the rate, a burst of 10 requests is still served; beyond that, refusal (503, or the code from limit_req_status, for example 429). deny address; refuses an address (403). Spreading load across several copies of an app: an upstream name {"{"} server 127.0.0.1:3001 weight=9; server 127.0.0.1:3002 weight=1; {"}"} block in the http block (a file from sites-enabled is included there), then proxy_pass http://name/; weight gives each one's share (here 9 visits out of 10 for the first), down sets it aside, backup is only used if the others don't answer. A server that doesn't answer is skipped; a page that answers 500 reaches the visitor as is.
    .synopsis = nginx -t ; nginx -T ; nginx -v
    .option-t = checks the configuration (test) and stops.
    .option-T = checks, then displays the whole configuration read, file by file.
    .option-v = displays the version.
    .example-1 = check the configuration before reloading
    .example-2 = reread the whole configuration, includes and all
    .example-3 = the nginx version
man-pg_dump = backs up a PostgreSQL database while it runs, consistently: everything it contained at a given moment (copying the files of /var/lib/postgresql while the database is writing does not guarantee that). With no option, writes SQL; -Fc writes an archive in "custom" format, to be read back with pg_restore (which can restore just one table). -f gives the file (written with the rights of the account running pg_dump), -t takes only one table.
    .synopsis = pg_dump [-Fc] [-f file] [-t table] database
    .example-1 = an archive of the ludo database, to read back with pg_restore
    .example-2 = the database's SQL, on screen
man-pg_restore = restores an archive written by pg_dump -Fc into a database (-d), which must exist (createdb). -l lists what the archive contains, -t restores only one table, --data-only (-a) only the data (into tables that already exist), --clean (-c) first deletes what exists. A dump in text format is read back with psql -f.
    .synopsis = pg_restore [-l] [-d database] [-t table] [--data-only] [--clean] archive
    .example-1 = what the archive contains
    .example-2 = restore it into a test database
    .example-3 = put back only the rows of the prets table
man-ping = sends small messages (ICMP "echo") to a machine and measures the round-trip time. Here, 4 sends by default (on Linux, ping keeps going until Ctrl+C). Beware: many servers don't answer ping, without being down for all that.
    .synopsis = ping [-c N] machine
man-ps = lists processes, that is, the programs currently running (process status). With no option: yours; "ps aux": everyone's, with their share of processor (%CPU) and memory (%MEM). "ps -ef": everyone's too, with each process's parent (PPID), the one that started it. "ps -u account": those of one account. A process marked <defunct> is a zombie: finished, but its parent hasn't read its result yet.
    .synopsis = ps [aux] ; ps -ef ; ps -u account
    .example-1 = everyone's processes
    .example-2 = with each one's parent
    .example-3 = marc's processes
man-psql = the PostgreSQL client: sends an SQL query to a database and displays the result. You connect under your account's name: for the database administrator, sudo -u postgres psql. -d picks the database, -c gives the query (in quotes), -f reads a file of SQL (a text-format dump, for example), -l lists the databases. A few commands: \l (the databases), \dt (the tables), \d table (its columns), SELECT count(*) FROM table; (how many rows). Beware, DELETE FROM table; with no WHERE deletes ALL the rows.
    .synopsis = psql [-d database] [-c query | -f file] ; psql -l
    .example-1 = the list of databases
    .example-2 = the tables of the ludo database
    .example-3 = how many rows in the prets table
man-pwd = displays the full path of the place where you are (print working directory).
    .synopsis = pwd
man-readlink = displays what a symbolic link points to. -f gives the full path, following every link: useful to know which version is live behind a "current" link.
    .synopsis = readlink [-f] link
    .option-f = the full path, all links followed (canonicalize).
    .example-1 = where the link points
    .example-2 = the full path, links followed
man-restic = backs up directories into an encrypted repository, as snapshots: each backup keeps the full state of the moment, but only new data takes up space (deduplication). You need the repository (-r) and its password (--password-file, otherwise it asks): without the password, nothing is readable, not even the list. snapshots lists the snapshots; ls, find, diff and dump look inside; restore ID --target directory recreates the files (with their full path) under that directory, --include to take only part of them; forget removes snapshots according to a policy (--keep-daily 7…) or by their ID, without freeing space: prune is what deletes the data no longer needed (or forget --prune); check verifies the repository.
    .synopsis = restic -r repository [--password-file file] snapshots|ls ID [directory]|find pattern|diff ID ID|dump ID file|restore ID --target directory [--include path]|backup directory|forget [--keep-daily N --keep-weekly N --keep-monthly N] [--prune] [--dry-run]|prune|check|stats
    .example-1 = the list of snapshots
    .example-2 = which snapshots contain this file, with its size and date
    .example-3 = what changed between two snapshots
    .example-4 = display a file as it was in a snapshot
    .example-5 = restore a single file, under /tmp/restore
    .example-6 = keep 7 days, 4 weeks, 12 months, and free the space (try with --dry-run first)
    .example-7 = check the repository
    .command-1 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password snapshots
    .command-2 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password find --long journal.txt
    .command-3 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password diff 1a2b3c4d 5e6f7a8b
    .command-4 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password dump latest /home/you/collection/journal.txt
    .command-5 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password restore 1a2b3c4d --target /tmp/restore --include /home/you/collection/journal.txt
    .command-6 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password forget --keep-daily 7 --keep-weekly 4 --keep-monthly 12 --prune
    .command-7 = restic -r /mnt/townhall/repo --password-file ~/.config/restic/password check
man-rndc = controls the running DNS server named (BIND). rndc reload rereads the zone files (all of them, or just one); named then notifies the secondary servers (NOTIFY), which only copy the zone again if its serial number (in the SOA) has increased. The named log (journalctl -u named) says what was loaded. Restricted to root.
    .synopsis = rndc reload [zone] ; rndc status
    .example-1 = reload a zone after editing it (and increasing its serial)
    .example-2 = the server's status
man-rm = deletes a file (remove). Beware: rm goes through no bin, it's permanent. (The trash of graphical desktops, ~/.local/share/Trash, is just a folder: a file goes there with gio trash, never with rm.)
    .synopsis = rm file
    .example-1 = delete a file, forever
man-rsnapshot = keeps snapshots of one or more directories: the full state of the moment, at regular intervals. Files that haven't changed from one snapshot to the next are not copied again but linked (hard links: the same file under two names, see ls -i); each snapshot looks complete, but only the changes take up space. Everything is set in /etc/rsnapshot.conf, with TABS between the items (never spaces): snapshot_root (where to store the snapshots, with a trailing slash), retain name count (a level, from most frequent to rarest, and how many to keep: retain daily 7, then retain weekly 4), backup source destination (backup /srv/share/ localhost/). rsnapshot configtest checks the file. rsnapshot daily rotates the daily level: daily.0 is the most recent, the oldest is deleted; a rarer level (weekly) takes over the oldest daily. -t shows what would be done, without doing anything. rsnapshot du measures the real space used. You run it with cron: the rarest level a little before the most frequent.
    .synopsis = rsnapshot [-t] [-v] [-c file] configtest | du | level
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = see what a run of the daily level would do, without doing anything
    .example-3 = rotate the daily level
    .example-4 = the space taken by each snapshot
    .example-5 = a crontab line: every night at 3:30 a.m.
man-scp = copies files from one machine to another, over ssh (secure copy): the same keys, the same ~/.ssh/config (ProxyJump included). You write the other machine in front of the path: machine:path (a relative path starts from the home of the remote account). -r copies a whole directory, -P picks the port, -J goes through a gateway. For many files, or to copy only what changed, rsync is better suited. To avoid: FTP, which sends passwords and files in clear text (prefer scp, sftp, or FTPS if the host requires it).
    .synopsis = scp [-r] [-P port] [-J gateway] source… [account@]machine:destination ; scp [account@]machine:source destination
    .example-1 = send a file into the home of the remote account
    .example-2 = fetch an archive from the wiki machine
    .example-3 = send a whole directory, going through the gateway
man-sh = runs a script: a file of commands, one per line (the shell /bin/sh, dash on Debian). A script starts with #!/bin/sh; once made executable (chmod +x), it can also be run by its path: ./deployer.sh. In a script: # comments, NAME=value creates a variable read with $NAME, $(command) is replaced by what the command writes, set -e stops everything at the first command that fails, exit 1 ends in failure. "a && b" runs b only if a succeeded, "a || b" only if it failed. In this game, no if, for or while: one command per line.
    .synopsis = sh script [argument...]
    .example-1 = run a script
    .example-2 = run an executable script by its path
    .example-3 = make it executable
man-sort = sorts lines: alphabetically, or -n numerically (2 before 10), -r in reverse.
    .synopsis = sort [-n] [-r] [file]
    .option-n = sorts numbers by value (2 before 10).
    .option-r = sorts in reverse.
    .example-1 = from smallest to largest
man-ssh = connects to another machine, encrypted (Secure Shell). ssh reads ~/.ssh/config to find the machine's real name, the user and the key to use, then checks the machine's identity in ~/.ssh/known_hosts. The first time, it asks you to confirm its fingerprint (yes). You authenticate with a private key, which must be readable only by you (chmod 600), whose public key is in ~/.ssh/authorized_keys of the remote account; otherwise, if the server allows it, with a password. exit closes the connection. -J goes through an intermediate machine (a bastion, or gateway) to reach an internal machine you can't reach directly; ProxyJump does the same in ~/.ssh/config. -L port:machine:port opens a tunnel: the port on your machine leads to the service as seen from the remote machine (localhost: the remote machine itself); with -f -N, ssh keeps it open in the background without opening a session. A command after the machine name runs over there and gives control back.
    .synopsis = ssh [-G] [-i key] [-p port] [-J gateway] [-L port:machine:port] [-f] [-N] [user@]machine [command]
    .option-i = uses this private key file (identity file).
    .option-p = port to contact (22 by default).
    .option-J = goes through this machine to reach the destination (jump host).
    .option-L = tunnel: a local port leads to a service on the other side (local forwarding).
    .option-N = no session or command: only the tunnels.
    .option-f = goes to the background after connecting (with -N, for a tunnel).
    .option-G = displays the configuration that would apply (from ~/.ssh/config), without connecting.
    .example-1 = connect to the machine kingdom (name defined in ~/.ssh/config)
    .example-2 = the same without ~/.ssh/config: user, full name and key
    .example-3 = see the machines you know
    .example-4 = reach an internal machine through the gateway
    .example-5 = a tunnel to the admin page of an internal machine
    .example-6 = run a command on the remote machine
man-ssh-copy-id = installs your public key on another machine: it adds it to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys of the remote account (creating ~/.ssh with the right permissions). You have to authenticate one last time another way (the password); after that, the key is enough, and you can close password access (PasswordAuthentication no in sshd_config). By default, it takes the most recent key in ~/.ssh; -i picks one.
    .synopsis = ssh-copy-id [-i file] [account@]machine
    .example-1 = install your key on the community machine
    .example-2 = install a specific key
man-ssh-keygen = creates and manages SSH keys. With no option, or with -t ed25519, it creates a key pair: the private key (~/.ssh/id_ed25519, to keep to yourself, chmod 600) and the public key (.pub, to give to servers, in their ~/.ssh/authorized_keys; ssh-copy-id does that). A passphrase protects the private key if you lose it. -C adds a comment (often account@machine), -f picks the file, -N gives the passphrase without asking for it. It also manages the list of known machines (~/.ssh/known_hosts): when a machine has been reinstalled, its host key changes and ssh refuses to connect to it; after checking the new fingerprint by some other means, you remove the old key with -R, under the exact name written in known_hosts.
    .synopsis = ssh-keygen [-t ed25519] [-C comment] [-f file] [-N passphrase] ; ssh-keygen -R machine [-f file] ; ssh-keygen -F machine ; ssh-keygen -l -f file
    .option-t = the key type (here, ed25519).
    .option-C = a comment, written at the end of the public key.
    .option-N = the passphrase (empty: none), without asking for it.
    .option-R = removes all keys of this machine from known_hosts (the old file is kept as .old).
    .option-F = looks for this machine in known_hosts and displays its lines.
    .option-l = displays the fingerprint of the keys in a file (with -f).
    .option-f = the file to read or modify (by default ~/.ssh/known_hosts).
    .example-1 = forget the machine's old key (after checking the new fingerprint!)
    .example-2 = is the machine in known_hosts?
    .example-3 = the fingerprints of all known machines
    .example-4 = create an ed25519 key pair
man-sudo = runs a command as root, the administrator who has all rights (superuser do). Asks for YOUR password, not root's. With -u account, the command runs under an account other than root (for example postgres, for the database). Who may do what is written in /etc/sudoers and /etc/sudoers.d (man sudoers); sudo -l lists what you can do, sudo -l -U account what another account can do.
    .synopsis = sudo [-u account] command ; sudo -l [-U account]
    .example-1 = read a file restricted to root
    .example-2 = list the databases, as postgres
    .example-3 = what you are allowed to do with sudo
    .example-4 = what lina is allowed to do with sudo
man-sudoers = the sudo policy: who can run what, under which account. /etc/sudoers also reads every file in /etc/sudoers.d (@includedir line), EXCEPT those whose name contains a dot or ends with ~: "till" is read, "till.conf" is ignored. A rule: "who machines=(account) commands", for example "%till ALL=(root) /usr/bin/systemctl restart till" (the till group, on all machines, as root, that command only). who: an account, %group, or ALL. Commands are written with their full path (which systemctl); with no arguments, all arguments are allowed; ALL allows everything. NOPASSWD: in front of the commands, sudo doesn't ask for a password. A single syntax error, and sudo refuses everything: always edit these files with visudo, which checks before saving.
    .synopsis = /etc/sudoers ; /etc/sudoers.d/
    .example-1 = the main policy
    .example-2 = the included files
    .example-3 = write a rule in its own file, checked before being saved
man-visudo = edits /etc/sudoers (or, with -f, another rules file, such as /etc/sudoers.d/till) checking the syntax before saving: an error in sudoers would block sudo for everyone. You edit a copy; if it has an error, visudo shows it and asks "What now?": e to go back to editing, x to quit without saving anything, Q to save anyway (danger). -c checks the policy without modifying anything. The saved file belongs to root, with mode 0440.
    .synopsis = visudo [-c] [-f file]
    .option-c = only checks (check), without modifying anything.
    .option-f = the rules file to edit (file).
    .example-1 = edit the main policy
    .example-2 = write rules in a file of /etc/sudoers.d
    .example-3 = check the whole policy
man-systemctl = manages services, those programs that run all the time (web server…), through systemd. status shows a service's state and its latest messages; start, stop, restart start it, stop it or restart it; reload makes it reread its configuration. enable makes it start with the machine, disable doesn't: start only lasts until the next reboot. --failed lists failed services. Only root can change a service's state. list-timers lists the timers (systemd's scheduled tasks) and when they next fire. To modify a service without touching the shipped file (in /usr/lib/systemd/system), you write an override (drop-in): edit opens it in the editor (/etc/systemd/system/service.service.d/override.conf), with the {"["}Service] section and only the lines to change, for example MemoryMax=512M (the memory beyond which systemd kills the service) or Restart=on-failure (restart it if it dies in failure). After writing an override by hand, daemon-reload makes systemd reread it. set-property changes a property right away and keeps it: systemctl set-property service MemoryMax=512M.
    .synopsis = systemctl [status|start|stop|restart|reload|enable|disable|is-active|is-enabled|edit] service ; systemctl set-property service property=value ; systemctl daemon-reload ; systemctl --failed ; systemctl reboot
    .example-1 = the state of nginx and its latest messages
    .example-2 = the failed services (the first reflex after a reboot)
    .example-3 = restart nginx
    .example-4 = start nginx now AND at every boot of the machine
    .example-5 = write an override for a service (e.g. MemoryMax=, Restart=)
    .example-6 = limit a service's memory right away
man-systemd-analyze = tools for systemd; here, calendar tests an OnCalendar= expression (a timer's): its normalized form, and when it will next fire. Format: day of the week (optional), date (*-*-*: every day), time H:M:S. *:0/5 every 5 minutes; *-*-* 03:30:00 every day at 3:30 a.m.; Mon..Fri 08:00 on weekdays; hourly, daily, weekly. To check a timer before enabling it.
    .synopsis = systemd-analyze calendar EXPRESSION…
    .example-1 = every 5 minutes
    .example-2 = every day at 3:30 a.m.
man-systemd_timer = a systemd timer (NAME.timer, in /etc/systemd/system) runs a service (NAME.service, often Type=oneshot) at a fixed time or at intervals: the modern equivalent of a crontab line, with the journal as a bonus (journalctl -u NAME). In the {"["}Timer] section: OnCalendar= a time (*:0/5 every 5 minutes, daily…, to test with systemd-analyze calendar), or OnBootSec= (after boot) with OnUnitActiveSec= (after the last run: on its own, it never fires, since the service has never run yet). In {"["}Install], WantedBy=timers.target, so that it can be enabled. Then: systemctl daemon-reload, systemctl enable --now NAME.timer, and systemctl list-timers to check.
    .synopsis = /etc/systemd/system/NAME.timer and NAME.service
    .example-1 = write the timer
    .example-2 = enable and start it
    .example-3 = the list of timers, with when they next fire
man-tail = displays the end of a file (the last 10 lines, or N). Handy for logs, where the most recent is at the end. With -f, tail does not stop: it prints the lines as they are added, until Ctrl-C. That is how you watch a server live.
    .synopsis = tail [-n N] [-f] [file]
    .option-n = number of lines to display (10 by default): tail -n 3 file.
    .option-f = follows the file: prints what gets added, until Ctrl-C.
    .option-F = like -f, and keeps going if the file is replaced (log rotation).
    .example-1 = the 5 most recent events
    .example-2 = watch the visits arrive live (Ctrl-C to stop)
man-tar = archives (tape archive): -c creates an archive from files and places, -t lists its content, -x extracts it; -z compresses (gzip); -f gives the archive's name. The stored paths are relative: -x recreates them where you are, or in the place given by -C. When extracted by root, an archive keeps the owners and permissions of its files. E.g.: tar -czf sauvegarde.tar.gz dossier
    .synopsis = tar -c|-t|-x [-z] -f archive [-C place] [places...]
    .option-c = creates an archive (create).
    .option-t = lists the content of an archive.
    .option-x = extracts an archive (extract).
    .option-z = compresses or decompresses with gzip.
    .option-v = displays the files processed (verbose).
    .option-f = the archive's name follows (file).
    .option-C = extracts into this place instead of the current place (change directory).
    .example-1 = archive and compress
    .example-2 = see what's inside (and check it can be read to the end)
    .example-3 = extract here
    .example-4 = restore a backup to its place, from the root
man-tee = copies what comes through a pipe both to the screen and into a file (like a T-junction). -a appends to the end instead of overwriting. With sudo, it's root that writes the file.
    .synopsis = tee [-a] file
    .option-a = appends to the end of the file instead of overwriting it.
man-test = checks a condition, without displaying anything: succeeds if it is true, fails otherwise. -f file (a file exists), -d place (a place exists), -e (either one), -s (non-empty file), -x (executable), -n text (non-empty), -z text (empty), a = b, a != b, and for numbers -eq, -ne, -lt, -gt. In a script, you often write [ condition ] (with spaces around the brackets): it's the same command. With || exit 1, a script stops if the condition is false.
    .synopsis = test condition ; [ condition ]
    .example-1 = does index.html exist?
    .example-2 = the same, with brackets (in a script)
    .example-3 = stop the script if the file is missing
man-timedatectl = shows and sets the machine's time (systemd). With no argument (status): the local time, universal time (UTC), the time zone, whether the clock is synchronized (System clock synchronized) and whether the NTP service is running. set-timezone changes the time zone (list-timezones lists them; Europe/Paris for France); services that schedule things (cron) are then restarted. set-ntp true enables and starts systemd-timesyncd, which keeps the clock on time thanks to an NTP server (Network Time Protocol), chosen with NTP= in /etc/systemd/timesyncd.conf (reread when the service starts). timesync-status shows the server being queried and whether it answers. set-time sets the time by hand (refused if NTP is active). Settings are restricted to root.
    .synopsis = timedatectl [status] ; timedatectl set-timezone zone ; timedatectl set-ntp true|false ; timedatectl timesync-status ; timedatectl list-timezones ; timedatectl set-time "YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS"
    .example-1 = the time, the time zone and the NTP state
    .example-2 = the time zones of Europe
    .example-3 = switch to Paris time
    .example-4 = enable NTP synchronization
    .example-5 = the NTP server and its answer
man-top = the processes using the most resources, with the machine's state at the top: how long it has been running (up), the tasks (zombies included), the processor and the memory. By default, sorted by %CPU; -o %MEM sorts by memory. Columns: RES, the memory actually used (in KiB, or m, g); S, the state (S sleeping, R running, Z zombie). To track a memory leak, run top again and compare RES. The real top refreshes every 3 seconds until q; here, it shows a snapshot (like top -b -n 1).
    .synopsis = top [-b] [-n N] [-o field] [-c]
    .option-b = "batch" mode: displays and gives control back (for a script).
    .option-n = number of updates before stopping.
    .option-o = sorts by this field: %MEM, %CPU, RES, PID.
    .option-c = the full command line instead of the program name.
    .example-1 = the most memory-hungry processes
    .example-2 = a snapshot, for a script
man-touch = creates an empty file (or updates its date if it already exists).
    .synopsis = touch file
    .example-1 = create an empty file
man-traceroute = shows the path of packets, router by router ("hop" by "hop"), with each one's response time. "* * *": that hop didn't answer.
    .synopsis = traceroute machine
man-truncate = changes the size of a file: truncate -s 0 empties it without deleting it. The program writing it can carry on (at the same spot), and the space is freed right away, unlike rm on a file that is still open.
    .synopsis = truncate -s size file...
    .example-1 = empty a huge log without stopping the program
man-umount = detaches a mounted file system (unmount). You give it the mount point or the device. Impossible while you are inside it ("target is busy"): leave the directory first. Restricted to root.
    .synopsis = umount mount_point | device
    .example-1 = detach the USB stick
    .command-1 = sudo umount /mnt/usb
man-uniq = merges identical lines that follow each other. -c shows how many times each one appears. You sort first (sort) so that duplicates follow each other: sort | uniq -c
    .synopsis = uniq [-c] [file]
    .option-c = counts how many times each line repeats.
    .example-1 = count the repetitions, then sort
man-wc = counts (word count) lines (-l), words (-w) and bytes (-c). With no option: all three.
    .synopsis = wc [-l] [-w] [-c] [file]
    .option-l = counts lines.
    .option-w = counts words.
    .option-c = counts bytes.
    .example-1 = how many lines contain "erreur"?
man-whatis = displays in one line what a command is for.
    .synopsis = whatis command
man-whoami = displays which user name you are acting as (who am I).
    .synopsis = whoami
man-hostname = displays the name of the machine you are on (read from /etc/hostname). Useful when you hop from one machine to another with ssh: the prompt shows it too (you@name).
    .synopsis = hostname [-f]
    .option-f = the full name (FQDN), from /etc/hosts.
    .example-1 = which machine am I on?
    .example-2 = its full name, with the domain

man-check_disk = monitoring plugin (monitoring-plugins, the Nagios and Icinga format): the free space on a volume. It writes one line (DISK OK, WARNING or CRITICAL, then the measurements after "|") and returns a code: 0 OK, 1 WARNING, 2 CRITICAL, 3 UNKNOWN. -w 20%: warning when less than 20% is free; -c 10%: critical below 10%. -W and -K do the same for free inodes (without them, inodes are not monitored). -p picks the volume (you can give several). On Debian, the plugins are in /usr/lib/nagios/plugins, outside the PATH: you run them by their full path.
    .synopsis = /usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_disk -w FREE% -c FREE% [-W FREE% -K FREE%] -p directory [-p directory…]
    .example-1 = the system disk: warning below 20% free, critical below 10%
    .example-2 = the data volume, inodes included
man-check_http = monitoring plugin: does a website or web service answer? -H the machine (or -I its address), -p the port, -u the path. HTTP OK for a 2xx or 3xx response, WARNING for 4xx, CRITICAL for 5xx or if nobody answers (Connection refused). Return codes: 0, 1, 2, 3 (UNKNOWN), like all plugins.
    .synopsis = /usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_http -H machine [-p port] [-u path]
    .example-1 = is the radio's live stream answering?
man-check_procs = monitoring plugin: counts processes (those of one command with -C) and alerts outside a range: -c 1: wants at least 1 process (critical otherwise), -w :10 at most 10, -c 1:1 exactly 1. Return codes: 0, 1, 2, 3, like all plugins.
    .synopsis = /usr/lib/nagios/plugins/check_procs [-C command] [-w RANGE] [-c RANGE]
    .example-1 = is the stream relay running?
man-chgrp = changes the group of a file or directory (change group). The owner can choose a group they belong to; root can choose any group. -R also acts on the whole content of a directory.
    .synopsis = chgrp [-R] group file...
    .option-R = acts on the directory and everything it contains (recursive).
    .example-1 = give the file to the web-team group
    .example-2 = a whole directory, with its content
    .command-2 = sudo chgrp -R web-team /srv/team
man-chown = changes the owner of a file (change owner), and its group with owner:group. You can give names or numbers (uid:gid), even a number that no account on the machine has. Only root can do it. -R also acts on the whole content of a directory.
    .synopsis = chown [-R] owner[:group] file...
    .option-R = acts on the directory and everything it contains (recursive).
    .example-1 = give a file to malo
    .example-2 = owner and group at once, on a whole directory
    .example-3 = by numbers: uid 1001, gid 1001
man-groups = displays the groups an account belongs to: its primary group, then its secondary groups.
    .synopsis = groups [user...]
    .example-1 = my groups
    .example-2 = malo's groups
man-host = the short version of dig: a name's addresses and mail servers, in sentences. A second argument picks the DNS server to query.
    .synopsis = host name [server]
    .example-1 = a name's addresses
    .example-2 = the same question to another server
man-ip = shows and changes the network configuration (iproute2): interfaces (ip link), their addresses (ip addr, or ip a), routes (ip route). -br gives one line per interface; -d adds details (for a VLAN: its 802.1Q number). ip link add link eth1 name eth1.10 type vlan id 10 creates the interface for VLAN 10 on eth1; ip addr add gives it an address; ip link set … up brings it up. These changes take effect right away, but disappear on reboot: to make them last, you write them in /etc/network/interfaces (man interfaces). Changing the configuration is restricted to root. -c (color) adds colours: interface names in cyan, state UP in green and DOWN in red, addresses in magenta.
    .synopsis = ip [-br] [-d] [-c] addr|link|route [show|add|del|set] …
    .example-1 = the interfaces and their addresses
    .example-2 = the same, one line per interface
    .example-3 = the details of an interface (the VLAN number)
    .example-4 = create the interface for VLAN 10 on eth1
    .example-5 = give it an address
    .example-6 = bring it up
    .example-7 = the routes (including the default gateway)
man-ifup = brings up an interface according to /etc/network/interfaces (ifupdown, on Debian): creates the VLAN interface if needed, gives it its address, its gateway. -a brings up all interfaces marked "auto" (as at boot). An interface already brought up by ifup is not brought up again: ifdown first. Restricted to root.
    .synopsis = ifup [-a] [interface...]
    .example-1 = bring up everything marked "auto"
    .example-2 = bring up VLAN 10
man-ifdown = takes down an interface brought up by ifup (and deletes a VLAN interface). Restricted to root.
    .synopsis = ifdown [-a] [interface...]
    .example-1 = take down VLAN 10
man-interfaces = Debian's persistent network configuration (/etc/network/interfaces), read at boot and by ifup. "auto eth1.10": to bring up at boot; "iface eth1.10 inet static" opens the description of an interface, followed by its indented options: address (10.10.10.1/24, or address and netmask), gateway (the default gateway). An interface named eth1.10 is VLAN 10 of eth1 (vlan package); with another name, vlan-raw-device eth1 says on which interface. "inet manual": the interface is brought up without an address (a trunk, for example). source reads other files (/etc/network/interfaces.d/*).
    .synopsis = /etc/network/interfaces
    .example-1 = the current configuration
    .example-2 = edit it
man-dnsmasq = a small DHCP (and DNS) server for a local network. It reads /etc/dnsmasq.conf and the files in /etc/dnsmasq.d, one option per line: interface= (where to listen; eth1* means eth1 and everything starting with eth1), except-interface= (where never to listen), dhcp-range=start,end[,lease-time] (a range of addresses; dnsmasq picks the one that matches the network of the interface where the request arrives), dhcp-host=MAC,name,address (reserve an address for a device), dhcp-option= (gateway, DNS: by default, the router's address). --test checks the configuration. The log (journalctl -u dnsmasq) shows each exchange (DHCPDISCOVER, OFFER, REQUEST, ACK) and /var/lib/misc/dnsmasq.leases the leases given out.
    .synopsis = dnsmasq --test ; /etc/dnsmasq.d/*
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = reread it (restart the service)
    .example-3 = the addresses given out
    .example-4 = the log of exchanges
man-nft = configures the kernel firewall (nftables). Rules live in tables (family ip, ip6, or inet for both); a table holds chains; a "base" chain hooks into one stage of the packets' journey (hook input: to the machine itself; forward: through it, from one network to another; output: from it; postrouting: just before leaving) and has a policy (policy accept or drop) for whatever no rule has decided. A rule reads from left to right: conditions (iifname "eth1.10": came in through this interface; oifname: will leave through that one; ip saddr, ip daddr: addresses, or networks 10.10.20.0/24; tcp dport 443; ct state established,related: a reply to an exchange already allowed), then actions (counter counts, log writes to the kernel log, accept, drop, reject, jump to another chain). NAT is done in a chain of type nat: masquerade (at the postrouting hook) replaces the source address with the one of the outgoing interface; dnat to 10.10.40.10 (at the prerouting hook, priority dstnat) replaces the destination, before routing: the forward chain already sees the new address. A set is written between braces: iifname "{"{"} "eth1.10", "eth1.20" {"}"}". nft -f reads a whole file and applies it in one go (nothing changes if there is an error); on Debian, /etc/nftables.conf is loaded at boot by the nftables service if it is enabled (systemctl enable nftables). What you type with nft add only lasts until the next reboot. On a machine run by ufw and fail2ban, nft list ruleset shows the rules they write (table ip filter, ufw-* chains; table inet f2b-table): in the game, you look at them there without touching them. Root only.
    .synopsis = nft [-c] [-a] -f file ; nft [-a] list ruleset|tables|table family name|chain family table chain ; nft add|insert|delete … ; nft flush ruleset
    .option-f = reads the commands from this file, and applies them all together.
    .option-c = only checks, without applying anything.
    .option-a = shows the number (handle) of each rule, so you can delete it.
    .example-1 = the rules loaded right now, with their counters
    .example-2 = check the file without loading it
    .example-3 = load the file (like systemctl restart nftables)
    .example-4 = add a rule at the end of a chain (until the next reboot)
    .example-5 = the rules of a chain, with their numbers
    .example-6 = delete rule number 7
man-sysctl = reads and changes kernel settings, stored in /proc/sys (net.ipv4.ip_forward is the file /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward). net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1 lets the machine pass packets from one network to another (route them); at 0, it only keeps those addressed to it. -w changes a value right away, but the kernel forgets everything on reboot: to make a setting last, you write it in a file in /etc/sysctl.d (man sysctl.d), which systemd-sysctl applies at boot; sysctl --system applies them without waiting. Changing a value is reserved for root.
    .synopsis = sysctl [-n] key... ; sysctl -w key=value ; sysctl -p [file] ; sysctl --system ; sysctl -a
    .option-w = changes the value (write).
    .option-p = applies the settings from a file (/etc/sysctl.conf by default).
    .option-a = shows all settings (all).
    .option-n = shows only the value, without the name.
    .example-1 = is routing enabled?
    .example-2 = enable it right away (until the next reboot)
    .example-3 = apply all the settings files, as at boot
    .example-4 = the same value, read from /proc/sys
man-sysctl_d = the kernel settings applied at boot: .conf files in /etc/sysctl.d (then /etc/sysctl.conf), read in the order of their names, one "key = value" line per setting (net.ipv4.ip_forward = 1). Lines starting with # or ; are comments. The last file that sets a value wins. sysctl --system applies them without rebooting.
    .synopsis = /etc/sysctl.d/*.conf ; /etc/sysctl.conf
    .example-1 = the settings files
    .example-2 = write your own
man-conntrack = shows the kernel's connection tracking table: each exchange going through the machine, with the outgoing direction (src=… dst=…) and the expected reply. [UNREPLIED]: no reply has come back; [ASSURED]: the exchange is established in both directions. If the reply is expected towards an address other than the original one, address translation (NAT) has taken place. An exchange blocked by the firewall does not appear. Root only.
    .synopsis = conntrack -L [-p proto] [-s address] [-d address] ; conntrack -C
    .option-L = lists the tracked exchanges (list).
    .option-C = counts the tracked exchanges (count).
    .option-p = only this protocol (tcp, udp).
    .option-s = only exchanges coming from this address (source).
    .option-d = only exchanges going to this address (destination).
    .example-1 = all current exchanges
    .example-2 = those of one device
    .example-3 = how many there are
man-wg = the WireGuard tool, a VPN: an encrypted tunnel between two machines, which recognize each other by keys (like ssh). wg genkey creates a private key (keep it secret: umask 077 first), wg pubkey derives the public key from it (to give to the other side). wg show displays the interfaces, their peers and, for each one, the latest handshake: without it, the two sides have not recognized each other. Root only, except genkey and pubkey.
    .synopsis = wg genkey ; wg pubkey < private-key ; wg show [interface] ; wg showconf interface
    .example-1 = first: files created afterwards will be readable only by you
    .example-2 = create the private key, and derive the public key from it
    .example-3 = the public key, to give to the peers
    .example-4 = the interfaces and their peers (handshake, transfers)
man-wg-quick = brings a WireGuard interface up or down from its file /etc/wireguard/NAME.conf. The [Interface] section gives the address inside the tunnel (Address), the UDP port to listen on (ListenPort) and the private key (PrivateKey); each [Peer] section describes a peer: its public key (PublicKey), the addresses it is allowed to use (AllowedIPs, a /32 address for a laptop), and, to reach it, its address and port (Endpoint). Warns if the file is world-readable. The wg-quick@NAME service does the same at boot (systemctl enable wg-quick@wg0). Root only.
    .synopsis = wg-quick up|down|strip interface
    .example-1 = bring wg0 up from /etc/wireguard/wg0.conf
    .example-2 = bring it down
    .example-3 = the same at boot, through the service
man-ldapsearch = searches an LDAP directory. -x: simple authentication (without SASL); without -D, anonymously (you can read everything except passwords). The filter says which entries: (uid=sami), (memberUid=camille), (objectClass=posixAccount), and combinations (&(…)(…)), (|(…)(…)), (!(…)); after the filter, the attributes to display (dn alone, uid uidNumber…). -b gives the base to search from (default: BASE in /etc/ldap/ldap.conf); -LLL shows the entries without comments. Answers are in LDIF format.
    .synopsis = ldapsearch -x [-LLL] [-b base] [-D dn -W] [filter] [attributes...] ; sudo ldapsearch -Y EXTERNAL -H ldapi:/// …
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind), not with SASL.
    .option-L = fewer comments (-LLL: the entries only).
    .option-b = the search base (the top of the branch).
    .option-s = the scope: base (the entry alone), one (its children), sub (the whole branch, the default).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as (bind DN).
    .option-W = prompt for the password.
    .option-w = the password, on the command line (it stays in the history: avoid).
    .option-H = the server address: ldap://localhost, or ldapi:/// (the local socket).
    .option-Y = the SASL mechanism: EXTERNAL, with ldapi:///, presents the Unix account (root: administrator on Debian).
    .example-1 = Malik's entry
    .example-2 = the groups Camille is still a member of
    .example-3 = the account numbers (to pick a free one)
    .example-4 = as root, without a password
man-ldapadd = adds entries to an LDAP directory, described in an LDIF file (-f): each entry starts with its dn: line, followed by its objectClass lines and its attributes; a blank line separates two entries. You must authenticate (-D and -W, or -Y EXTERNAL as root): anonymously, nothing gets written. Classes require certain attributes (inetOrgPerson: cn and sn; posixAccount: uid, uidNumber, gidNumber, homeDirectory); if one is missing, slapd rejects the entry (Object class violation). ldapadd is ldapmodify -a.
    .synopsis = ldapadd -x -D dn -W -f file.ldif ; sudo ldapadd -Y EXTERNAL -H ldapi:/// -f file.ldif
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as.
    .option-W = prompt for the password.
    .option-w = the password, on the command line.
    .option-f = the LDIF file to read.
    .option-H = the server address.
    .option-Y = the SASL mechanism (EXTERNAL with ldapi:///).
    .option-c = continue after an error.
    .example-1 = add the entry described in sami.ldif, as administrator
    .example-2 = the same as root, without a password
man-ldapmodify = modifies entries of an LDAP directory, from an LDIF file: after the dn: line, "changetype: modify", then changes "add: attribute", "replace: attribute" or "delete: attribute", each followed by its values and separated from the next by a "-" line. "changetype: add" adds an entry, "changetype: delete" deletes it. To remove someone from a group: delete: memberUid, then the value.
    .synopsis = ldapmodify -x -D dn -W -f file.ldif
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as.
    .option-W = prompt for the password.
    .option-w = the password, on the command line.
    .option-f = the LDIF file to read.
    .option-H = the server address.
    .option-Y = the SASL mechanism (EXTERNAL with ldapi:///).
    .option-a = an entry without changetype is an addition (like ldapadd).
    .option-c = continue after an error.
    .example-1 = apply the changes in groups.ldif
man-ldapdelete = deletes entries from an LDAP directory, by their DN. An entry that has children cannot be deleted. Careful: groups that mention the person (memberUid) are not updated.
    .synopsis = ldapdelete -x -D dn -W dn-to-delete...
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as.
    .option-W = prompt for the password.
    .option-w = the password, on the command line.
    .option-H = the server address.
    .option-Y = the SASL mechanism (EXTERNAL with ldapi:///).
    .example-1 = delete Camille's entry
man-ldappasswd = changes the password of an LDAP entry; the server stores it as a hash ({"{"}SSHA{"}"}). -S prompts for the new password (twice); without -S or -s, ldappasswd makes one up and displays it. A person can change their own (by authenticating with their own DN); the administrator can change everyone's.
    .synopsis = ldappasswd -x -D dn -W [-S | -s new] [target-dn]
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as.
    .option-W = prompt for the password (to authenticate).
    .option-w = the password to authenticate with, on the command line.
    .option-S = prompt for the new password.
    .option-s = the new password, on the command line.
    .example-1 = the administrator gives Sami a password
    .example-2 = Sami changes their own
man-ldapwhoami = tells you which identity the directory knows you by: useful to check that a password works.
    .synopsis = ldapwhoami -x [-D dn -W]
    .option-x = authenticate with a name and a password (simple bind).
    .option-D = the DN to authenticate as.
    .option-W = prompt for the password.
    .option-w = the password, on the command line.
    .example-1 = does Sami's password work?
man-slappasswd = computes the hash of a password, to put in userPassword (in an LDIF) instead of the plain-text password: {"{"}SSHA{"}"} followed by the salted hash. The same password gives a different hash every time (the salt changes).
    .synopsis = slappasswd [-s password]
    .option-s = the password (otherwise it is prompted for twice).
    .example-1 = prompt for the password, and show its hash
    .example-2 = the same, with the password on the line (it stays in the history)
man-slapcat = writes the whole content of the directory in LDIF format, straight from the database: it is the directory's backup (slapadd loads it back). Root only.
    .synopsis = slapcat
    .example-1 = the whole directory
    .example-2 = back it up to a file
man-ldif = the text format of LDAP entries (LDAP Data Interchange Format). An entry: "dn:" then one "attribute: value" line per value; a blank line between two entries; lines starting with # are comments, and those starting with a space continue the previous one. For changes: "changetype: add | modify | delete" after the dn, and in a modify, blocks "add:", "replace:", "delete:" separated by "-".
    .synopsis = file.ldif
    .example-1 = an example entry
man-freeradius = the RADIUS server: it answers the enterprise Wi-Fi access points (and switches) that ask it whether a person may get in (Access-Request), with yes (Access-Accept, with attributes: the VLAN, for example) or no (Access-Reject). Its configuration is in /etc/freeradius/3.0: clients.conf (the access points allowed to ask questions: their address, ipaddr, and the shared secret, secret), mods-enabled/ (the active modules: links to mods-available/, including ldap), mods-config/files/authorize (the "users" file: DEFAULT entries with conditions such as Ldap-Group == "offices", and replies such as Tunnel-Private-Group-Id = 10; the first one that matches wins). It only reads its configuration at startup: after a change, systemctl restart freeradius. -XC checks the configuration; -X runs the server in the foreground and narrates each request (the service must be stopped first).
    .synopsis = freeradius -XC ; freeradius -X
    .option-X = in the foreground, narrating everything (debug).
    .option-C = check the configuration, then stop.
    .example-1 = check the configuration
    .example-2 = stop the service…
    .example-3 = … to watch the server handle the access point's requests
    .example-4 = reload the configuration (the service)
    .example-5 = the requests received, accepted or rejected
man-radtest = sends an access request to a RADIUS server, as an access point would: a name, a password, the server, a port number on the access point (0), and the client's shared secret (localhost has testing123 in clients.conf). Shows the reply: Access-Accept and its attributes, or Access-Reject.
    .synopsis = radtest user password server nas-port secret
    .example-1 = can Sami get in, and into which VLAN?
man-sftp = transfers files over SSH, interactively: once connected (same keys, same ~/.ssh/config shortcuts as ssh), you type commands at the sftp> prompt: ls, cd, pwd for the remote machine; lls, lcd, lpwd for your own; get to fetch, put to send (with -r: a whole directory); mkdir, rm, rename; bye to quit. Everything travels encrypted, like ssh. Handy for looking before taking; for a one-shot copy, use scp or rsync.
    .synopsis = sftp [-P port] [-J jump] [-i key] [account@]machine[:directory]
    .option-P = the SSH port, if it is not 22.
    .option-J = go through another machine (like ssh -J).
    .option-i = the private key to use.
    .example-1 = connect to the wiki (through the ~/.ssh/config shortcut)
    .example-2 = (at the sftp> prompt) fetch the site's whole directory
    .example-3 = (at the prompt) send a file
    .example-4 = (at the prompt) the list of files on this side
    .example-5 = (at the prompt) quit
man-lftp = a command-line FTP client, the one you use with a shared hosting service that only offers FTP. By default, lftp encrypts the connection (FTPS: AUTH TLS, setting ftp:ssl-allow) and checks the server's certificate (ssl:verify-certificate): the name of the machine you ask for must be the one in the certificate (often the hosting provider's name, not your site's). Never turn these settings off: without encryption, the password travels in plain text. mirror copies a whole directory: from the hosting to you by default, from you to the hosting with -R (reverse); --delete removes on the other side what no longer exists, --dry-run shows what would be done without doing it. -u account prompts for the password; -e runs commands then stays open, -c runs them then quits.
    .synopsis = lftp [-u account] [-e "commands"] [-c "commands"] machine ; at the prompt: mirror [-R] [--delete] [--dry-run] [source [target]], ls, cd, lcd, put, get, set, bye
    .option-u = the account (the password is prompted for; "account,password" puts it in the history: avoid).
    .option-e = commands to run, separated by semicolons, before handing control back.
    .option-c = commands to run, then quit.
    .example-1 = connect to the hosting (the password is prompted for)
    .example-2 = (at the prompt) see what publishing would send, without sending anything
    .example-3 = (at the prompt) publish the site directory to public_html, removing what is no longer there
    .example-4 = the same in one go
man-id = prints an account's identity: its number (uid), its primary group (gid) and all its groups. A file's permissions are decided with this information.
    .synopsis = id [user]
    .example-1 = who am I, and in which groups?
    .example-2 = malo's identity
man-usermod = modifies an account (user modify), in particular its groups. -G gives the list of supplementary groups, separated by commas: it REPLACES the current groups. With -a (append), the groups are ADDED. -L locks an account's PASSWORD, -U unlocks it. Careful: -L does not prevent logging in with an SSH key; to close the account, you must also expire it (-e 1, as the real manual says). -s changes the login shell (/usr/sbin/nologin: no more sessions). Root only.
    .synopsis = usermod [-a] -G group[,group...] user ; usermod -L|-U user ; usermod -e date user ; usermod -s shell user
    .option-a = adds the groups from -G instead of replacing the current groups (append).
    .option-G = the list of supplementary groups, separated by commas.
    .option-L = locks the account's password (lock).
    .option-U = unlocks the account's password (unlock).
    .option-e = the account's expiry date (YYYY-MM-DD; 1: expired since 1970; empty: never).
    .option-s = the account's login shell.
    .example-1 = add malo to the adm group, without touching their other groups
    .example-2 = careful: malo is now only in adm (their other supplementary groups are removed)
    .example-3 = lock malo's password
    .example-4 = expire marc's account: no more logins at all, not even with a key
    .example-5 = no more sessions for marc (the account stays, and so do the files)
man-userdel = deletes an account (user delete), and removes it from its groups. With -r, its home directory and mailbox go too; its other files remain, owned by a number (uid) with no account. Refuses as long as a process of the account is running (-f forces it). Its crontab no longer runs (cron ignores unknown accounts). Before deleting an account, look at what it was running. Root only.
    .synopsis = userdel [-r] [-f] account
    .option-r = also removes the home directory and mailbox (remove).
    .option-f = even if the account is still in use (force).
    .example-1 = delete marc's account, keeping their files
    .example-2 = delete the account and its home directory
man-gpasswd = manages the members of a group: -a adds an account to the group, -d removes it (delete). Removing someone from the sudo group takes sudo away from them (on Debian). Root only.
    .synopsis = gpasswd -a account group ; gpasswd -d account group
    .option-a = adds the account to the group (add).
    .option-d = removes the account from the group (delete).
    .example-1 = remove marc from the sudo group
    .example-2 = add lina to the till group
man-pkill = stops processes chosen by their name or their account, without looking up their numbers: pkill pattern (the pattern is searched for in the program name), pkill -u account (all of the account's processes). Prints nothing if it found some (exit code 0), and exit code 1 if it found none. You can only stop your own processes, or any of them as root.
    .synopsis = pkill [-u account] [pattern]
    .option-u = only the processes of this account (user).
    .example-1 = stop all of marc's processes (and close their sessions)
    .example-2 = stop the processes named tail
man-which = gives the full path of a command: the first file with that name in the PATH directories (/usr/local/bin, /usr/bin…). Useful where the full path is required, as in sudoers or a crontab. Prints nothing (exit code 1) for a shell builtin (cd, umask…).
    .synopsis = which command…
    .example-1 = where is systemctl?
    .example-2 = where is visudo? (in /usr/sbin, with the administration commands)
man-who = lists the sessions open on the machine: who, on which terminal (pts), since when, and from which address. A session lasts as long as its sshd process is running.
    .synopsis = who
    .example-1 = who is logged in right now?
man-history = displays the history: the commands typed on this machine, numbered. The shell can run them again without retyping: !! runs the last one again (sudo !! runs it again with sudo, when you forgot sudo), !12 command number 12, !-2 the one before last, !cat the last one starting with cat. The expanded line is shown before it runs. The ↑ and ↓ arrows and Ctrl-R go through the same history. A line starting with a space is not kept. A shell built-in (help history, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = history [N] | history -c
    .option-c = clears the history.
    .example-1 = all the commands typed, numbered
    .example-2 = the last 5
    .example-3 = find an ssh command typed earlier
    .example-4 = run the last command again
    .example-5 = run it again with sudo ("Permission denied"? sudo !!)
    .example-6 = run command number 12 again
man-umask = shows or changes the session's mask (umask): the permissions REMOVED from the files and directories you create. A file starts from 666 (rw-rw-rw-), a directory from 777, minus the mask: with 022 (the default), a file is rw-r--r-- and the group cannot write to it; with 002, it is rw-rw-r--. The change only applies to the session: to keep it, write umask 002 in ~/.profile. Shell builtin (help umask, on a real Linux).
    .synopsis = umask [-S] [mask]
    .option-S = shows the allowed permissions, in symbolic form (u=rwx,g=rx,o=rx).
    .example-1 = the current mask
    .example-2 = the permissions it leaves, in letters
    .example-3 = the group will be able to write to what I create (for this session)

man-journalctl = reads the systemd journal, where services write their messages (and their errors). -u keeps only one service, -n the last N lines, -b the current boot, -p err the errors, -k the kernel messages (like dmesg). --since and --until keep a time period ("2027-09-12 02:00", today, yesterday, -1h). -x adds explanations and -e jumps to the end: journalctl -xeu nginx is the classic formula when a service refuses to start. -f follows the journal live, until Ctrl-C. You must be root, or in the adm or systemd-journal group, to read everything.
    .synopsis = journalctl [-u service] [-n N] [-b] [-k] [-p priority] [--since DATE] [--until DATE] [-x] [-e] [-f]
    .option-k = only kernel messages, since boot (kernel).
    .option-u = only the messages of this service (unit).
    .option-n = only the last N lines.
    .option-b = only since the machine's last boot (boot).
    .option-p = only this priority and more severe ones: err, warning, info…
    .option-x = adds explanations to known messages.
    .option-e = jumps straight to the end of the journal.
    .option-f = follows the journal: the last 10 lines, then whatever arrives, until Ctrl-C.
    .example-1 = nginx's messages
    .example-2 = the formula when a service won't start
    .example-3 = errors since the last boot
    .example-4 = the last 20 messages
    .example-5 = everything that happened between 2:00 and 3:30 on a given night
    .example-6 = watch the ssh connections live (Ctrl-C to stop)
man-reboot = restarts the machine. Enabled services (systemctl enable) start again on their own; the others stay stopped. Over an ssh connection, the session is cut: you have to reconnect. Root only.
    .synopsis = reboot
    .example-1 = restart the machine

man-named-checkzone = checks a BIND zone file before loading it: the syntax, the addresses, the SOA. Prints the serial number it read and OK, or the errors with the file and line. In a zone file, a name that does not end with a dot is relative to the zone: www becomes www.cafe.example., but cafe.example (without a dot) becomes cafe.example.cafe.example.; @ stands for the zone itself.
    .synopsis = named-checkzone zone file
    .example-1 = check the zone before rndc reload
man-named-checkconf = checks the configuration of named (BIND): says nothing if it is correct.
    .synopsis = named-checkconf
    .example-1 = check the configuration
man-nano = a simple text editor, in the terminal. You type directly into the file; the shortcuts are at the bottom of the screen, ^ meaning Ctrl: ^O (Ctrl+O) writes the file, ^X (Ctrl+X) quits and offers to save the changes. To edit a file owned by root, open nano with sudo. (In the game, on mobile, the shortcuts are also buttons.)
    .synopsis = nano file
    .example-1 = write notes (the file is created if it does not exist)
    .example-2 = edit a configuration file owned by root

man-sshd = the SSH server: it is the one that accepts (or refuses) ssh connections. You don't start it by hand: systemd takes care of it (ssh service). But two options are used every day, with sudo: -t checks the configuration and says nothing if it is correct; -T shows the complete configuration as sshd would apply it, once all the files are read. After a change: sudo sshd -t, then sudo systemctl reload ssh.
    .synopsis = sshd [-t | -T] [-f file]
    .option-t = checks the configuration (test): says nothing if all is well, otherwise gives the file and line of the error.
    .option-T = shows the effective configuration, value by value (in lowercase).
    .option-f = reads this file instead of /etc/ssh/sshd_config.
    .example-1 = check the configuration before reloading the service
    .example-2 = see the value of PasswordAuthentication actually applied
man-ssh_config = the SSH client's configuration file, ~/.ssh/config: shortcuts for the machines you connect to. Each block starts with "Host alias" (* wildcards are allowed) and its lines apply when you type ssh alias. For each option, the FIRST value found wins: specific blocks first, "Host *" at the end. Useful options: HostName (the real name or address, as seen by the machine the connection starts from), User, Port, IdentityFile (the key to use), ProxyJump (go through a gateway, like ssh -J: the destination machine's name is then looked up by the gateway). ssh -G alias shows the configuration that would apply.
    .synopsis = ~/.ssh/config
    .example-1 = add or edit a shortcut
    .example-2 = see the options ssh would apply for wiki
man-sshd_config = the SSH server's configuration file: one option per line, "Keyword value". Lines starting with # are comments (often the default value). Careful: for each keyword, the FIRST value read is the one that counts, and the Include line reads other files at the place where it appears (on Debian, /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf, at the very top). Useful options: PermitRootLogin no (no direct root login), PasswordAuthentication no (keys only), PubkeyAuthentication yes, AllowUsers (the only accounts allowed), Port (the listening port, 22 by default; the only exception to the first-value rule: you can repeat Port, and sshd then listens on each one). Changes only apply after sudo systemctl reload ssh; sessions already open are never cut.
    .synopsis = /etc/ssh/sshd_config
    .example-1 = edit the configuration
    .example-2 = the included files, which come BEFORE the rest of the main file

man-ufw = manages the machine's firewall (Uncomplicated Firewall): which INCOMING connections are accepted. You set a default policy (the safest: deny everything incoming), then allow the useful doors one by one, by port number (22/tcp), by service name (ssh, http) or by application profile (OpenSSH, "Nginx Full"). The first rule that matches decides. limit accepts but slows down connections that come too close together (against brute force). Before enable, allow SSH: otherwise, no new connections at all. Root only.
    .synopsis = ufw status [verbose|numbered] ; ufw enable|disable ; ufw default allow|deny|reject [incoming|outgoing] ; ufw allow|deny|reject|limit RULE ; ufw delete RULE|NUMBER ; ufw app list
    .example-1 = the firewall's status, default policies and rules
    .example-2 = allow SSH (application profile)
    .example-3 = allow SSH only from one address
    .example-4 = deny everything incoming by default
    .example-5 = enable the firewall (after allowing SSH!)
    .example-6 = delete a rule
man-ss = shows the machine's "sockets": the ports where a program waits for connections, and the established connections (socket statistics). -t TCP, -u UDP, -l only what is listening, -a everything, -n port numbers rather than their names, -p the program (you must be root to see other accounts' programs). An address 0.0.0.0 means "on all interfaces", so reachable from the network; 127.0.0.1, only from the machine itself.
    .synopsis = ss [-t] [-u] [-l] [-a] [-n] [-p]
    .option-t = TCP sockets.
    .option-u = UDP sockets.
    .option-l = only listening sockets (listening).
    .option-a = all sockets, listening and established.
    .option-n = port numbers, without translating them into service names.
    .option-p = the program using each socket.
    .example-1 = who is listening, on which address and which port?
    .example-2 = established TCP connections

man-fail2ban-client = controls fail2ban, the guard that reads logs and bans addresses that fail to log in too often (brute force). A "jail" watches a service: sshd for SSH. Other jails watch other logs: nginx-limit-req reads the rejections from nginx's rate limiting, recidive reads fail2ban's own log and bans for a long time the addresses already banned several times. status gives the list of jails; status sshd, the failures, the log being read and the banned addresses; set sshd unbanip ADDRESS lifts a ban in one jail, unban ADDRESS in all of them; get sshd bantime (or findtime, maxretry, ignoreip, logpath) reads a loaded setting; -t checks the configuration; reload reloads it. Root only.
    .synopsis = fail2ban-client status [jail] ; fail2ban-client set jail banip|unbanip ADDRESS ; fail2ban-client get jail bantime|findtime|maxretry|ignoreip ; fail2ban-client -t ; fail2ban-client reload
    .example-1 = the active jails
    .example-2 = the failures and banned addresses of the sshd jail
    .example-3 = unban an address in the sshd jail
    .example-4 = unban an address in all jails
    .example-5 = the ban time loaded for the recidive jail
    .example-6 = check the configuration
    .example-7 = reload the configuration
man-jail_conf = the fail2ban configuration. Do not edit /etc/fail2ban/jail.conf, which an update would replace: write your settings in /etc/fail2ban/jail.local (or jail.d/*.local), read AFTERWARDS and which take precedence. Format: sections ([DEFAULT] for everything, [sshd] for the SSH jail) and "key = value" lines. enabled = true enables a jail; maxretry: number of failures before the ban; findtime: the window in which they are counted; bantime: the ban duration (600, 10m, 1h, 1d, 1w; -1 for ever). A jail is enabled in ITS OWN section (enabled = true in [recidive], for example): put in [DEFAULT], enabled would enable every jail in jail.conf, and fail2ban would refuse to start because it cannot find their logs. logpath: the log being read; banaction: what does the blocking (banaction_allports: all ports). ignoreip: the addresses or networks (CIDR, e.g. 192.0.2.0/24) never banned, separated by spaces; port: the ports closed to banned addresses (ssh, 2222, or a list: ssh,2222). A value written in a jail's section always wins over [DEFAULT], whatever the file: the [sshd] in jail.conf says "port = ssh", so a different port is set in an [sshd] section of jail.local.
    .synopsis = /etc/fail2ban/jail.local
    .example-1 = write your settings
    .example-2 = the value applied
    .example-3 = what the jails block, in the firewall

man-rsync = copies files and directories, copying only what has changed: the classic backup tool. -a (archive) copies a whole directory while keeping permissions; -v shows what is copied; -n (--dry-run) shows what WOULD be done, without touching anything; --delete removes from the destination what is no longer in the source (an exact mirror: be careful, it copies deletions too). Mind the trailing slash: "rsync -a memories/ dest/" copies the CONTENT of memories into dest, "rsync -a memories dest/" creates dest/memories. To or from another machine, write machine:path (rsync then goes through ssh, with the same keys).
    .synopsis = rsync [-a] [-v] [-n] [--delete] source... destination ; rsync -a source... [account@]machine:destination
    .option-a = archive mode: recursive, keeping permissions and dates.
    .option-r = recursive: also copies the contents of directories.
    .option-v = verbose: lists what is copied (verbose).
    .option-n = trial run: shows what would be done, without writing anything (dry run).
    .example-1 = back up the content of one directory into another
    .example-2 = try first, with --delete: nothing is touched
    .example-3 = restore a single file from the backup
    .example-4 = publish a site to another machine (over ssh)
    .command-1 = rsync -av ~/memories/ /mnt/backup/memories/
    .command-2 = rsync -avn --delete ~/memories/ /mnt/backup/memories/
    .command-3 = rsync -a /mnt/backup/memories/first-letter.txt ~/memories/
man-crontab = manages your scheduled tasks: commands that cron runs on its own, at set times. crontab -e opens your crontab in the editor; when you close it, it is checked and then installed. crontab -l displays it, crontab -r deletes it. One line = five time fields, then the command: minute (0-59), hour (0-23), day of month (1-31), month (1-12), day of week (0-7, 0 and 7 = Sunday). * means "every", 1-5 a range, 1,15 a list, */10 "every 10". Or a shortcut: @daily (every night at midnight), @hourly, @weekly, @reboot (at boot). Tasks run from your home directory; whatever they output is sent to you by mail. root can view and edit another account's crontab with -u: sudo crontab -u malo -l.
    .synopsis = crontab [-u account] -e | -l | -r | file
    .example-1 = edit your scheduled tasks
    .example-2 = display them
    .example-3 = a crontab line: every night at 3:15
    .example-4 = another account's crontab
    .command-3 = 15 3 * * * rsync -a ~/memories/ /mnt/backup/memories/

## Manual layout and messages from man-db and coreutils

# Header of each page, between "LS(1)" and "LS(1)"
manual-header = Game Manual
manual-section-name = NAME
manual-section-synopsis = SYNOPSIS
manual-section-description = DESCRIPTION
manual-section-options = OPTIONS
manual-section-examples = EXAMPLES
manual-section-see-also = SEE ALSO
# First line of "command --help"
manual-use = Usage: { $synopsis }
manual-complete = Full manual: man { $command }
manual-option-invalid =
    { $command }: invalid option -- '{ $letter }'
    Try '{ $command } --help' for more information.
# An unknown long option (--frobnicate), as getopt prints it in coreutils. { $option }: the option typed
manual-option-long-unknown =
    { $command }: unrecognized option '{ $option }'
    Try '{ $command } --help' for more information.

man-nmap = the network explorer: it sends packets to a machine (or a whole range of addresses) and says, for each TCP port, whether it is open (a program answers), closed (the machine answers "nobody here") or filtered (a firewall drops the packet: no answer at all). The ports are "seen from the internet": that is what an attacker, or an auditor, sees. -sV also asks the version of the service that answers (useful to spot outdated software); -p chooses the ports (-p 22,80; -p 1-1024; -p- for all of them); -sn only says which machines answer; -Pn ignores a missing answer to ping; -oN keeps the result in a file (evidence to attach to the report); -F looks only at the most common ports; -n does not look up names. Use nmap only on what is in your letter of engagement: probing a machine you have no right to probe is a fault, sometimes a crime. 127.0.0.1 is the machine itself, seen from the inside (the firewall does not apply there).
    .synopsis = nmap [options] target...  (a name, an address, a network 192.0.2.0/29, a range 192.0.2.20-25)
    .option-sV = the version of the service listening on each open port.
    .option-sn = only say which machines answer, without scanning the ports.
    .option-Pn = do not first test whether the machine answers (it may hide its answer to ping).
    .option-n = do not look up the names of machines.
    .option-F = only the most common ports (100) instead of the usual 1000.
    .option-p = the ports to examine: 22,80 or 1-1024; -p- for all 65535.
    .option-oN = write the result, as you read it, into the given file.
    .example-1 = the open ports of a machine
    .example-2 = with versions, keeping the trace in a file
    .example-3 = only SSH, http and https
    .example-4 = does this machine answer?
    .example-5 = all ports, not just the most common ones
man-lynis = the security audit tool for a server. "lynis audit system" examines the machine it runs on (with sudo: it reads what only root can see) and sorts its remarks into warnings (to fix first) and suggestions (leads, of uneven seriousness), each with its test identifier (SSH-7408, PKGS-7392…) and an address that explains it. At the end, the hardening index sums everything up out of 100: it is for comparing before and after, not for chasing 100 at any price. Lynis changes nothing: it is up to you to decide, suggestion by suggestion, what is worth the effort. The details of each audit are kept in /var/log/lynis-report.dat (hardening_index=, warning[]=, suggestion[]=), which you read with grep.
    .synopsis = lynis audit system ; lynis show version
    .example-1 = the full audit of the machine
    .example-2 = only the suggestions of the last audit
    .example-3 = the hardening index of the last audit
    .example-4 = lynis's version
